insert into public.chunks (id, episode_id, cms_item_id, chunk_index, content, token_estimate, speakers, start_timestamp, end_timestamp, podcast_index, episode_name, slug, web_url, guest_name, guest_title, categories, short_description, podcast_length)
select id, episode_id, cms_item_id, chunk_index, content, token_estimate, speakers, start_timestamp, end_timestamp, podcast_index, episode_name, slug, web_url, guest_name, guest_title, categories, short_description, podcast_length
from jsonb_to_recordset($jsonpayload$
[{"id": "31fab3d7-5e8d-4eee-ad17-1536a4381298", "episode_id": "2255f9f3-9efa-4df1-8fab-364a54c4bc69", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4846", "chunk_index": 13, "content": "Maddie Petry (26:19): When you niche down, you have to ensure that it's laddering back to your brand mission and your brand goals and what you value as a brand. And if you start to get off that track too much, then it's probably not going to align overall. But when you have that niche within that trajectory, that funnel of, yeah, this is who we are, this is who we've always been, and this aligns exactly with what we're trying to accomplish as a brand, then you're going to find success in that.\n\nCole Heilborn (26:48): Okay. So that's interesting, and I feel like this kind of is a good segue to the next part of my questions where you're talking about understanding who the brand is. And the only way you can do that I'm assuming, is if you as a person, as an individual know who you are and the members on your team can understand who they are. As you're asking yourselves these complicated questions, you need to have something grounded in yourself. How do you work through that on an individual level with the questions you're asking about this large organization?", "token_estimate": 268, "speakers": ["Maddie Petry", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "26:19", "end_timestamp": "26:48", "podcast_index": 35, "episode_name": "Ep 35: Maddie Petry | Down the Mountain | Mammu‪t‬", "slug": "ep-35-maddie-petry-down-the-mountain-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-35-maddie-petry-down-the-mountain-mammut", "guest_name": "Maddie Petry is the Marketing Director", "guest_title": "Mareting Director, North America, Mammut", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"I think the challenge is playing the long game. It's easy for brands to really think [short term]. For us, it was a process of creating a space for our community to come to us.\"", "podcast_length": "44:57"}, {"id": "dadd440c-0615-4ae6-92ff-6b3403646eab", "episode_id": "2255f9f3-9efa-4df1-8fab-364a54c4bc69", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4846", "chunk_index": 14, "content": "Maddie Petry (27:24): It comes from experience, and generally people who are in the outdoor space do it because they're passionate about it and they experience the outdoors both from a newcomer level to an experience level. So for me in particular, I was very comfortable in the ski space. It's the space I know very well. My whole family's done it. I'm ingrained in it. I've done it since I was two. I competed in it and felt very confident in how I shopped as a consumer, how I experienced the outdoors as a consumer in that space. But when it came time to, I don't know why I chose to do this, but I signed up for an Ironman one year, and I had zero bike experience. I had zero swim experience. I was a very novice runner and decided to do this crazy thing. And I was at a very beginner level and I was seeking out any information and any education I could find on people who had been in my position and how brands helped bridge the gap of, this is the equipment you need, here's how we're going to reach you, here's what this looks like, here's some training options. (28:39): And I found for me in particular that there was just a lack of that, and it was really frustrating to me. I wanted to see more. I wanted to understand how people, even from the basics where they put their bottle cages on their bikes and what swimsuit they had to do laps in the pool, and it just coming from this very expert level to this very humbling, beginner level kind of touched, I guess it struck a chord with me, (29:08): And I felt like I could really relate to the people that were coming to the outdoors for the first time and weren't getting that information they needed, but also on the ski side was like, Hey, I'm an expert in this space and I need you to really step up what you're talking about so that it makes sense for me as I try to accomplish some of these bigger objectives. And so having that experience really allows you to go into these marketing situations and say, okay, I understand this sector and I understand this sector and I understand how I need to a bridge the gap, but also how to speak to both of these sectors in meaningful ways. And I think so many people experience that in the outdoors where you kind of silo in into one sector and say, yeah, I'm getting expert level here, but when you try something new, you're not sure what that's going to look like for you as a consumer or as a participant.", "token_estimate": 605, "speakers": ["Maddie Petry"], "start_timestamp": "27:24", "end_timestamp": "27:24", "podcast_index": 35, "episode_name": "Ep 35: Maddie Petry | Down the Mountain | Mammu‪t‬", "slug": "ep-35-maddie-petry-down-the-mountain-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-35-maddie-petry-down-the-mountain-mammut", "guest_name": "Maddie Petry is the Marketing Director", "guest_title": "Mareting Director, North America, Mammut", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"I think the challenge is playing the long game. It's easy for brands to really think [short term]. For us, it was a process of creating a space for our community to come to us.\"", "podcast_length": "44:57"}, {"id": "582f05db-9142-43e1-bdb4-ed201889831e", "episode_id": "2255f9f3-9efa-4df1-8fab-364a54c4bc69", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4846", "chunk_index": 15, "content": "Cole Heilborn (30:01): Yeah. Interesting. So after your Iron Man experience first, did you figure out where to put the water bottles?\n\nMaddie Petry (30:07): I figured it out, yep.\n\nCole Heilborn (30:09): Did you opt for on the frame or on the handlebars with the sip straw?\n\nMaddie Petry (30:14): I did the frame. Okay.\n\nCole Heilborn (30:16): Interesting.\n\nMaddie Petry (30:17): Off frame.\n\nCole Heilborn (30:18): Interesting. Yeah. Alright. Anyways, so after that experience, what did you take away from that? And did you learn anything that you could apply to your job at the time?\n\nMaddie Petry (30:30): Yeah, I think what I learned the most is as a consumer and as somebody participating, I just wanted to be heard and I wanted what I was thinking about and what worried me, what challenged me, what excited me to be heard by brands and to be incorporated into their marketing. And something that really, really impacted me within my role of saying, okay, we have to create a community and we have to create a space so that these people are heard. There's so many lessons and so many learning points to take away from this group of individuals that can have a really big impact, not only on this group, but all the groups in between and up to the expert, and how do we speak about our product? How do we use all these sectors of marketing to really make sure that we're addressing the needs of this consumer? And not just saying, yeah, yeah, just grab a jacket, go, good luck.\n\nCole Heilborn (31:25): Yeah. Yeah. Interesting. Okay. So what's a very simple example of you're describing from someone who's entirely new entering the sport. Let's imagine there's someone trying to get into skiing for the very first time and they were just trying to figure out where to start. How is moot being there and supporting that individual if they are in those sectors that you're targeting? What's an example of how you guys are reaching them in a simple, non overwhelming way?", "token_estimate": 493, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Maddie Petry"], "start_timestamp": "30:01", "end_timestamp": "31:25", "podcast_index": 35, "episode_name": "Ep 35: Maddie Petry | Down the Mountain | Mammu‪t‬", "slug": "ep-35-maddie-petry-down-the-mountain-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-35-maddie-petry-down-the-mountain-mammut", "guest_name": "Maddie Petry is the Marketing Director", "guest_title": "Mareting Director, North America, Mammut", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"I think the challenge is playing the long game. It's easy for brands to really think [short term]. For us, it was a process of creating a space for our community to come to us.\"", "podcast_length": "44:57"}, {"id": "c4bf3a39-72af-4797-be9b-c8f08328286c", "episode_id": "2255f9f3-9efa-4df1-8fab-364a54c4bc69", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4846", "chunk_index": 16, "content": "Maddie Petry (31:58): Yeah, I think for us, a lot of it, going back to that education conversation. So previously you've looked at a site, you see 20 down jackets, you don't know what's going to be the best down jacket for you to accomplish the job. So really taking that step back and educating the consumer of, Hey, if you are a resort skier, here's the options and the offerings we're going to offer you. Or if you're a resort skier, here's the options that we can offer you that's going to make you have a really great experience on the resort. And if we're a backcountry skier, here's the kids we recommend. Here's what our athletes use, here's what the professionals use, and this will allow you to what's going to be the best choice for you? And really breaking that down to everything from a jacket and a pant to what do you wear against your body and your base layers and what that looks like to even, how do you pack your bag for the day if you're going to be on the resort, it looks very different than a back country backpack and what does that look like? (33:01): And involving people that are professionals in this space. So it's not just a brand speaking to you, but it's people who do this every single day, and they're a trusted ally and they can tell you exactly what they do and how they dress and what their passion looks like, and using that tactic to really speak to these people.\n\nCole Heilborn (33:22): Yeah, no, that's great. Really making the education super simple. How were people finding that? Is that through, I don't know, articles and ranking PR results on Google so people can find that stuff? Is that in-person workshops at their local ski club? How do people even find that info that you're creating?", "token_estimate": 434, "speakers": ["Maddie Petry", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "31:58", "end_timestamp": "33:22", "podcast_index": 35, "episode_name": "Ep 35: Maddie Petry | Down the Mountain | Mammu‪t‬", "slug": "ep-35-maddie-petry-down-the-mountain-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-35-maddie-petry-down-the-mountain-mammut", "guest_name": "Maddie Petry is the Marketing Director", "guest_title": "Mareting Director, North America, Mammut", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"I think the challenge is playing the long game. It's easy for brands to really think [short term]. For us, it was a process of creating a space for our community to come to us.\"", "podcast_length": "44:57"}, {"id": "e8cf8da2-d112-4da1-8c78-4d5b4b00aeac", "episode_id": "2255f9f3-9efa-4df1-8fab-364a54c4bc69", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4846", "chunk_index": 17, "content": "Maddie Petry (33:45): Yeah, it's kind of an omnichannel approach to it. So through our email sends, through our website, through our professional groups, our resort partnerships, and then again in in-person events or activations where you really get that one-on-one time to really speak to those people and get very individual with what their needs are. So again, I think you have to come at it from a full scope and really approach it in all sorts of ways so that it really lands with the consumer,\n\nCole Heilborn (34:19): Knowing that you guys are out there trying to simplify the safety that goes into being outside. It can be very simple as things just like bring your ton essentials and know some basic first aid and some navigation, but it can get really complicated really quick. And everyone is kind of on their own, I guess, path to being safe in the outdoors. And I don't know that there's a lot, I mean, I shouldn't say that there are a lot of resources out there that are helping people do that, but I think it's great that you guys are bringing your 150 8-year-old experience to these people and trying to help us all figure out how to be a little safer.\n\nMaddie Petry (35:02): Yeah, it's really exciting. And again, as a consumer, this is the stuff that I want to see, and so being able to help people get a better understanding has been really exciting and something I really enjoy about my job.\n\nCole Heilborn (35:16): Yeah, no, that's cool. And it also means, I mean, as a marketer, you're obviously very always impacting people, but this is a way that you're impacting people hopefully for a really good reason. Saving a life, preventing an injury, those are all real world, very tangible benefits.\n\nMaddie Petry (35:36): Yeah, it's definitely meaningful and it definitely drives you to continue to be successful in these areas and continue to help our consumers.\n\nCole Heilborn (35:46): Yeah. Okay, last question. What's your hope for the brand as you guys continue to work into the next year and following years?", "token_estimate": 508, "speakers": ["Maddie Petry", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "33:45", "end_timestamp": "35:46", "podcast_index": 35, "episode_name": "Ep 35: Maddie Petry | Down the Mountain | Mammu‪t‬", "slug": "ep-35-maddie-petry-down-the-mountain-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-35-maddie-petry-down-the-mountain-mammut", "guest_name": "Maddie Petry is the Marketing Director", "guest_title": "Mareting Director, North America, Mammut", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"I think the challenge is playing the long game. It's easy for brands to really think [short term]. For us, it was a process of creating a space for our community to come to us.\"", "podcast_length": "44:57"}, {"id": "837498b1-77c7-4a71-b801-c552f79fe8dc", "episode_id": "2255f9f3-9efa-4df1-8fab-364a54c4bc69", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4846", "chunk_index": 18, "content": "Maddie Petry (35:57): I guess really continuing to understand what makes our consumers tick and pairing our history, our experience with responding to the culture in relevant ways. Things are changing, things are pivoting, and we never want to lose who we are, but we also want to meet the needs of those consumers and create a safe community where people can come and learn, where they can get educated, where they feel confident going outside. And that just is a process of continuing to learn and grow and be thoughtful as a brand. So that's my hope for the future is that, again, we create this space for people to have their needs met, have their needs met, and get them excited about being outside and gets them confident about being outside, whether they're just starting out or they have a huge objective in the mountains.\n\nCole Heilborn (36:53): Yeah. No, very cool. Well, Maddie, thank you so much. If people want to follow along with either you or the brand, where can people find what you're up to?\n\nMaddie Petry (37:02): So moot.com, but also I love Instagram, so at moot na,\n\nCole Heilborn (37:09): I appreciate you taking the time to chat and share your experience. It's just been cool to learn what you guys have been up to and keep up the great work, and hopefully 2020 doesn't throw you guys any more curve balls.\n\nMaddie Petry (37:19): Oh, right. Thanks so much.\n\nCole Heilborn (37:22): Alright, take care. Bye. Thanks again for listening to this episode. If you enjoyed it, please consider sharing it with a friend and leaving us a review on Apple. Also, remember to check out Darby Communications. If you're looking for a great PR and marketing team, go ahead and find 'em at darby communications.com.", "token_estimate": 431, "speakers": ["Maddie Petry", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "35:57", "end_timestamp": "37:22", "podcast_index": 35, "episode_name": "Ep 35: Maddie Petry | Down the Mountain | Mammu‪t‬", "slug": "ep-35-maddie-petry-down-the-mountain-mammut", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-35-maddie-petry-down-the-mountain-mammut", "guest_name": "Maddie Petry is the Marketing Director", "guest_title": "Mareting Director, North America, Mammut", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"I think the challenge is playing the long game. It's easy for brands to really think [short term]. For us, it was a process of creating a space for our community to come to us.\"", "podcast_length": "44:57"}, {"id": "e505be81-d8e1-4533-8b76-b3e10fb5f265", "episode_id": "173150f4-bcd8-4775-a2c4-504412f06ad0", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4847", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:02): Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Helebo and I'll be your host today. This podcast dives deep with leaders of the outdoor marketing industry as they discuss the gritty details of their work, personal challenges and latest adventures, whether you're standing in the lift line, shuttling to fresh loam, or cracking a local beer. I hope you enjoyed today's episode. This is the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. This episode is produced by Portside Productions, an outdoor, commercial, and film production company based in the Pacific Northwest. Before we jump into today's episode, I wanted to talk about Darby Communications. They're our presenting sponsor for this episode. I've gotten to know them over the last few months as they've helped me with some PR and marketing for this podcast. The Darby Comm team has designed their process around my needs, combining a people first mindset with an analytical data-driven approach. Today's business world moves fast, but the Darby Comm team has taken the time to truly get to know me and understand my goals. They've been a great partner to collaborate with, and I'm really impressed with their well-rounded approach that generate real results that actually help bring this podcast to more years. If you're in the market for a great PR team in the outdoor wellness and fitness industry, go ahead and check 'em out@darbycommunications.com. (01:20): Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. Today I'm sitting down with Linda Belfor, the VP of Marketing at Superfeet. Linda, how are you?\n\nLinda Balfour (01:27): I'm great. How are you doing today?\n\nCole Heilborn (01:28): Doing awesome. Enjoying this windy day and hoping that the power doesn't go out,\n\nLinda Balfour (01:32): Man. No kidding. I lost internet for a little bit this morning, which always makes work fun and exciting.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:38): Well, that's an excuse to read a book or get a second cup of coffee.\n\nLinda Balfour (01:43): Great. Totally work on some stuff where you, it's like being on an airplane for a couple of minutes, right? Where no one else can reach you so you can get some good focused work done.", "token_estimate": 545, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Linda Balfour"], "start_timestamp": "00:02", "end_timestamp": "01:43", "podcast_index": 36, "episode_name": "Ep 36: Linda Balfour | E-commerce Pivot | Superfee‪t‬", "slug": "ep-36-linda-balfour-e-commerce-pivot-superfeet", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-36-linda-balfour-e-commerce-pivot-superfeet", "guest_name": "Linda Balfour", "guest_title": "Superfeet", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"COVID brought us closer together and made us look at how we can do things more efficiently.\"", "podcast_length": "44:58"}, {"id": "eb072c32-9e5a-45fe-a2d2-629a3c22de19", "episode_id": "173150f4-bcd8-4775-a2c4-504412f06ad0", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4847", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Cole Heilborn (01:50): Yes, I like it. Well, you're a fellow Bellingham native, or not native, but you live here in Bellingham. There's a few of us who have been on the podcast who are actually from Bellingham, so you're one of the few.\n\nLinda Balfour (02:06): Yeah, I feel so lucky to be in Bellingham. I've been here for five years, actually moved up here for Superfeet, and when I was telling people that I was talking to Superfeet, they were telling me if I had a chance to live in Bellingham and have a job there when I showed up that I better take it. And so we listened to 'em and it's been an amazing move. It's a pretty special place.\n\nCole Heilborn (02:25): Yeah, no kidding. Especially, I mean, everywhere when you're Bellingham is close to Galbrath and the mountain biking and the running, but it sounds like you guys are closer than some, and man, that's got to be such a great way to just pop outside and get a few laps in or a few miles.\n\nLinda Balfour (02:41): It's incredible. Yeah, I got up this morning and snuck a run in between rain skulls, which was awesome. I think when we picked our house out, we were trying to decide if we wanted to live downtown or up closer to the trails, and our decision was to make it harder to get to bars and easier to get to trails, and so it's been working. I get out on trails I think probably at least six days a week, if not seven.\n\nCole Heilborn (03:04): Wow. Well, I guess that kind of makes sense considering your job in the company that you work for. Could you tell us a little bit more about Superfeet?", "token_estimate": 391, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Linda Balfour"], "start_timestamp": "01:50", "end_timestamp": "03:04", "podcast_index": 36, "episode_name": "Ep 36: Linda Balfour | E-commerce Pivot | Superfee‪t‬", "slug": "ep-36-linda-balfour-e-commerce-pivot-superfeet", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-36-linda-balfour-e-commerce-pivot-superfeet", "guest_name": "Linda Balfour", "guest_title": "Superfeet", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"COVID brought us closer together and made us look at how we can do things more efficiently.\"", "podcast_length": "44:58"}, {"id": "7cf4dad0-340b-4c7b-9380-3693907f5109", "episode_id": "173150f4-bcd8-4775-a2c4-504412f06ad0", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4847", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Linda Balfour (03:12): Yeah, so for those of you that don't know is we're best known as an insole company, but we like to talk about ourselves as a shape company. So we've been around since 1977. We make what you would probably recognize as the green insole or the blue insole, but we also have a lot of new technology that's out there that has 3D foot scanning and gate analysis. We have 3D printed personalized insoles, and all of it really is designed with the idea of giving people an affordable, easy option to be as comfortable as possible. So we had a lot of doctor referrals, people that have major foot pain, plantar fasciitis, tendonitis, stuff like that. But then we also make products for professional skiers and runners and people that just really want performance and great comfort out of their footwear.\n\nCole Heilborn (04:00): I think every person who is ran at least half a mile is probably familiar with your insoles because they make such a difference. It's pretty incredible.\n\nLinda Balfour (04:11): It's incredible. Yeah, we have 80% market share, 80 plus percent market share in the run specialty channel in the Outward channel, and then in the Trim tofi channel for ski. So yeah, all of those sports that really require you to be on your feet and give your feet take a beating Superfeet is there for you, and it makes a huge difference.\n\nCole Heilborn (04:33): Tell us a little bit more about your role, I guess, what does your job entail, but maybe before that, how'd you end up at Superfeet?", "token_estimate": 379, "speakers": ["Linda Balfour", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "03:12", "end_timestamp": "04:33", "podcast_index": 36, "episode_name": "Ep 36: Linda Balfour | E-commerce Pivot | Superfee‪t‬", "slug": "ep-36-linda-balfour-e-commerce-pivot-superfeet", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-36-linda-balfour-e-commerce-pivot-superfeet", "guest_name": "Linda Balfour", "guest_title": "Superfeet", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"COVID brought us closer together and made us look at how we can do things more efficiently.\"", "podcast_length": "44:58"}, {"id": "1c6f0a6a-5c40-4c74-b777-a9d4e75c8d1d", "episode_id": "173150f4-bcd8-4775-a2c4-504412f06ad0", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4847", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Linda Balfour (04:40): Yeah, so I ended up at Superfeet. I am a brand fan first and foremost. I'm a huge, I love love being outdoors. I love outdoor brands, and I love brands that really have a great strong ethic and do good things for the community and for people through their products. And so I came across Super Feet. I had been a user of Super Feet, but actually met the CMO of Superfeet through the Conservation Alliance, and we started talking about what an amazing brand Super Feet is and how they're a hundred percent employee owned and what they had been doing for the past 39 years at the time for the community and what they wanted to do in the future. And it just sounded like this perfect mix for me, this trifecta of amazing well loved product, really great innovation and a vision for what we could be, and then just a strong ethic and great community, a great community builder, which I think is really important to me. So those three things put together, plus when I came up here and met the team, just such a strong group of individuals and really fun people, it was hard to say no to.\n\nCole Heilborn (05:55): That's great. You guys are pretty involved with the Conservation Alliance, aren't you?\n\nLinda Balfour (05:59): Yeah, so super feet's a member of the Conservation Alliance, and we have been for, since probably the year before I started, so five or six years now, I've been really fortunate to be on the board of the Conservation Alliance for four of the years that I was at Super Feet and four years prior to that. So with that, we get to go out to Washington, DC and lobby on behalf of public lands. We do a lot in the community as far as getting employees outside and volunteering and doing trail work or helping with cleanup with the Whatcom Land Trust. So it is funding great work, but then also putting the manpower and the sweat equity behind building the community that we want both here and wherever we sell our products.\n\nCole Heilborn (06:43): Yeah, that's well said. So as the VP of marketing, what do you get to do?", "token_estimate": 514, "speakers": ["Linda Balfour", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "04:40", "end_timestamp": "06:43", "podcast_index": 36, "episode_name": "Ep 36: Linda Balfour | E-commerce Pivot | Superfee‪t‬", "slug": "ep-36-linda-balfour-e-commerce-pivot-superfeet", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-36-linda-balfour-e-commerce-pivot-superfeet", "guest_name": "Linda Balfour", "guest_title": "Superfeet", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"COVID brought us closer together and made us look at how we can do things more efficiently.\"", "podcast_length": "44:58"}, {"id": "5a510743-5486-46ba-9063-2ef476f70299", "episode_id": "173150f4-bcd8-4775-a2c4-504412f06ad0", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4847", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Linda Balfour (06:49): Oh, man. I get do all kinds of fun stuff. So my team manages all of our consumer and our retail marketing that ranges from what you see in store, like our merchandising in store, the signage in store to how we partner with our retailers to do advertising with some of 'em or how we put together promotions with 'em, all the way to what you see on social media from Superfeet, how we manage our website, and then how we show up on other people's websites. So it's kind of that breadth of advertising, pr, social media, retail, and then also really imagining who we want to be as a brand and what we stand for and how we internalize and externalize that.\n\nCole Heilborn (07:37): Okay, gotcha. Like most outdoor brands, you pretty much do a combination of a lot of things. I feel like that's the common denominator with everyone is that small teams, but you fight in the weight class above you.\n\nLinda Balfour (07:54): And the thing is it's a small team and we all do a lot, and gosh, I think I'd be bored if I worked at a company where I didn't get stuff.\n\nCole Heilborn (08:03): What would a job be where you only had to focus on one thing?\n\nLinda Balfour (08:05): Oh, man, I can't even imagine.\n\nCole Heilborn (08:09): So I know from our previous conversation that obviously COVID has brought on a number of different challenges for everyone, but for you guys, it sounds like it's kind of changed some of the things that you've been working on and expedited some of the systems and changes you were already making. We've seen this huge push, obviously as retail is starting to become an option. For a while there, it wasn't an option. You couldn't go to your local running store and pick up a pair of shoes. How has that, I guess, how has that affected you guys and what are you kind of working on today and I guess, what is it September? What's September look like for you?", "token_estimate": 475, "speakers": ["Linda Balfour", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "06:49", "end_timestamp": "08:09", "podcast_index": 36, "episode_name": "Ep 36: Linda Balfour | E-commerce Pivot | Superfee‪t‬", "slug": "ep-36-linda-balfour-e-commerce-pivot-superfeet", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-36-linda-balfour-e-commerce-pivot-superfeet", "guest_name": "Linda Balfour", "guest_title": "Superfeet", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"COVID brought us closer together and made us look at how we can do things more efficiently.\"", "podcast_length": "44:58"}, {"id": "5592fd84-774f-4eec-9cf6-e4b421aaba5b", "episode_id": "173150f4-bcd8-4775-a2c4-504412f06ad0", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4847", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Linda Balfour (08:51): Yeah, so COVID for so many companies, COVID had a really a big impact on Superfeet, and for me, it actually on a personal level, brought me a lot closer to the brand. So when COVID hit, we looked to our brand truths, and one of 'em is people first and our leadership team and our executive team really did everything that we could to put some measures in place really quickly to make sure that our employee owners were well cared for, that everybody was going to have a job at the end of the pandemic, and that we were really making some smart moves. And so some of that stuff was really difficult. It looked like short work weeks for some people and reduced budgets, and of course, you always get a reduced marketing budget when you get reduced budgets. So we really had to think about what it was that we were doing and how we were serving our customers and our consumers the best, and then also just making sure that we were taking care of each other. (09:51): So I think COVID on a personal level was amazing for Superfeet because it really brought us closer together and as we're, I wouldn't say coming out of it, but as we're looking forward, it also really did make us look hard and long at what it was that we were doing and what we wanted to do, and then how we could do some of it faster. So like I said, we were founded in 1977, and one of the things that drew me to the brand was that we really are, while we are this really trusted brand by people in the industry and well loved by our retailers, we're also a really innovative brand. And in some ways we operate a startup. And I think that was really, it started, that mindset started from our founders and has just stayed with the company all of these years. And so back at the very beginning, one of the reasons that Superfeet was founded was that our two founders had looked at the market and realized that 80% of people who needed orthotics had a similar kind of foot problem that could be solved by a similar shaped insole. (11:04): So they introduced the first orthotic grade over the counter insoles. Dr. S sch existed before that, but there was nothing that really did what you could get from a podiatrist when they introduced that. They also realized that with that a ton of education had to come along with it. So they spent a lot of time in ski shops and later on in outdoor stores and in running stores, educating retail staff about the benefits of an insole, why somebody would want it, how to introduce it, all those great things. And so that's a long way to say that over the past 43 years, 44 years, we've become a great expert on in-store retail and an expert on how to serve people. When you can actually look at their foot and put an insole underneath it, (11:54): It's not going to be a surprise to you, Cole, or to anybody that's listening that retail is amazing and specialty retail is amazing and it's really important, but shoppers are shopping differently, and they're shopping in retail, but especially with COVID, they're also shopping online a lot. And when you look at all of the studies that are happening right now, or if you just follow intuitively what you know, that change is not going backwards, right? Retail will always hold a place in people's hearts, and it will always be important brick and mortar retail, but online is going to become more and more essential for brands and for retailers as we move into the next phase of consumerism and shopping. So I'm now just talking, I don't even know where I started. So what we wanted to do, what I'm focused on right now, we've been focused on over the last couple of months and even before that was how is it that we take these expertise and this really amazing formula that we've created for brick and mortar and make those same tools, that education, that competence and purchasing the ability to buy a pair of insoles with any pair of shoes, how do we make that all possible online? (13:11): And it's a fun challenge, right? Because a lot of people don't understand insoles in the first place, and then you have to, how do you get them to one, even notice that they would want an insole, and then how do you get them to choose the right product? And then how do you give them the confidence that they have the right product and that they're going to be able to use it the way that it's supposed to be used?", "token_estimate": 1096, "speakers": ["Linda Balfour"], "start_timestamp": "08:51", "end_timestamp": "08:51", "podcast_index": 36, "episode_name": "Ep 36: Linda Balfour | E-commerce Pivot | Superfee‪t‬", "slug": "ep-36-linda-balfour-e-commerce-pivot-superfeet", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-36-linda-balfour-e-commerce-pivot-superfeet", "guest_name": "Linda Balfour", "guest_title": "Superfeet", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"COVID brought us closer together and made us look at how we can do things more efficiently.\"", "podcast_length": "44:58"}, {"id": "8d02319c-5fb5-4f2c-8aab-6501a1dbe626", "episode_id": "173150f4-bcd8-4775-a2c4-504412f06ad0", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4847", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Cole Heilborn (13:33): Right? Yeah. And I think what makes it maybe even more complicated is to understand, I think, how much goes into choosing the right insole. I know you guys have, I don't remember what it's called, but you've developed this software and these scanning tools that people, they could go into a retail shop, stand on this crazy cool machine, and it'll analyze your foot and then recommend an insole or make you a custom insole. So how do you take that level of customer service and then do that online? Do you ship everyone one of these fancy machines or?", "token_estimate": 144, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "13:33", "end_timestamp": "13:33", "podcast_index": 36, "episode_name": "Ep 36: Linda Balfour | E-commerce Pivot | Superfee‪t‬", "slug": "ep-36-linda-balfour-e-commerce-pivot-superfeet", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-36-linda-balfour-e-commerce-pivot-superfeet", "guest_name": "Linda Balfour", "guest_title": "Superfeet", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"COVID brought us closer together and made us look at how we can do things more efficiently.\"", "podcast_length": "44:58"}, {"id": "df39e999-f463-4e2d-87a7-02ebe1e9611b", "episode_id": "173150f4-bcd8-4775-a2c4-504412f06ad0", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4847", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Linda Balfour (14:10): Yeah, a hundred percent. So that in-store system, I think I am glad that you brought that up because that is, we call it at station, we've partnered with another company called Volume to create this joint solution, which is 3D scanning and gate analysis, and then it recommends and insole with the help of the sales associate, and those just rolled out, or they're in the process of rolling out all of the fleet feed doors across the United States. So hopefully by the end of the year we'll have more than 150 or 160 fit stations and specialty run and fleet feed doors. So that's really exciting, and it kind of is bringing tech and interest to the retail environment. But yeah, how do you take that if there's that much sophistication that needs to happen in store, how do you give people those tools online? And I think it's actually almost a little bit of the opposite. So we go back to Education 1 0 1 and simplifying the message, and even looking at the product mix that's out there and determining what the right products are for the right retailers. (15:15): Over the past couple of years, we've added to our heritage line, which is the green, the blue, the berry that everybody knows. We've added just a straight run series in Soul Line. That's really easy to know if you're going running that this is going to fit in a running shoe, or we have a Trailblazer series, so if you're going hiking, it's going to fit in a hiking shoe. So that was our first step, was really to take a look at our product mix and our offering and make it so that it's intuitive and you don't have to know all of the nuances of an insole, but you can choose a product that is going to be good for your use case. But we know that not everybody wants, there's a reason that all of those heritage products existed, and we want to get people into the right product. So we've actually started shifting internally a lot of our focus and taking people that were experts on the retail environment and retail merchandising and in-store training, and we focus them a lot on our retailer stores. So a lot of the stuff that we do is like, we'll test it on superfeet.com first and see if it's going to work, and then if it works, we build a toolkit, we roll those tools out to our retailers (16:27): With the idea that, Hey, we want to be the hub for the brand. We want everyone to come to superfeet.com. But then we also want every retailer to have every opportunity to get their shoppers insoles because they believe in us, they believe in insoles, they know it works. So how can we make sure that that connection happens?", "token_estimate": 655, "speakers": ["Linda Balfour"], "start_timestamp": "14:10", "end_timestamp": "14:10", "podcast_index": 36, "episode_name": "Ep 36: Linda Balfour | E-commerce Pivot | Superfee‪t‬", "slug": "ep-36-linda-balfour-e-commerce-pivot-superfeet", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-36-linda-balfour-e-commerce-pivot-superfeet", "guest_name": "Linda Balfour", "guest_title": "Superfeet", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"COVID brought us closer together and made us look at how we can do things more efficiently.\"", "podcast_length": "44:58"}, {"id": "ee0ae749-e17e-4a92-a896-95ca353d8ec3", "episode_id": "173150f4-bcd8-4775-a2c4-504412f06ad0", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4847", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Cole Heilborn (16:48): How do you help the consumer find the right insole when they're sitting on their computer?\n\nLinda Balfour (16:53): We have a number of tools that we're working on deploying right now, and a lot of 'em work together. So the first thing that we did, and our timing works out perfectly, is we launched an insole recommendation tool (17:07): Where you can go in and say, this is what I want to do in it. This is what my foot looks like. And it's real simple, right? It's like a picture of a flat arch, a picture of a middle, a regular arch, and a picture of a high arch, and you're like, my foot looks like it's a high arch and it's wide, and my shoes are roomy, or they're tight. And it's a series of a couple of really easy questions that then spits out a recommendation. That recommendation on our website we've already seen of our site average conversion rate, which was our average conversion, was about 3%. After adding this insole recommendation tool, we're getting the people that go through that tool an eight or a 9% conversion rate.\n\nSpeaker 3 (17:49): Whoa. So\n\nLinda Balfour (17:50): That idea intuitively that people need the guidance to get to the right product, it played itself out with that. So what we've done is while we can't take that exact package and put it on everybody's website, we're now in the process of creating a low tech version of that insole recommender that any of our retailers will have access to put on their sites. So that idea of test learn and then syndicate if we can,\n\nCole Heilborn (18:18): That's cool. And that's neat that you can kind of break the recommendation down into super simple, a few questions and then kind of give a very accurate recommendation. That's slick.", "token_estimate": 435, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Linda Balfour", "Speaker 3"], "start_timestamp": "16:48", "end_timestamp": "18:18", "podcast_index": 36, "episode_name": "Ep 36: Linda Balfour | E-commerce Pivot | Superfee‪t‬", "slug": "ep-36-linda-balfour-e-commerce-pivot-superfeet", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-36-linda-balfour-e-commerce-pivot-superfeet", "guest_name": "Linda Balfour", "guest_title": "Superfeet", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"COVID brought us closer together and made us look at how we can do things more efficiently.\"", "podcast_length": "44:58"}, {"id": "1c58c16b-49f9-4271-8127-36fc6be8b9fd", "episode_id": "173150f4-bcd8-4775-a2c4-504412f06ad0", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4847", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Linda Balfour (18:32): It's cool. Yeah, it's really cool. And then another stuff that we're doing, we actually today just went live with seven product videos on our website. By the end of the month, we'll have product videos for every single one of our products that aren't over technical, but they just tell you again what the product's for what you can expect it to feel like. So hopefully the combination of, I went through this recommendation tool and then I saw the video will help somebody get that confidence that it's the right product for them.\n\nSpeaker 3 (19:00): And\n\nLinda Balfour (19:00): Again, those will make sure that they're working on our site, and then as soon as they are, they're going to get packaged up and all of our retailers will be able to use them as well.\n\nCole Heilborn (19:09): So I think it's especially obvious in the outdoor space, but everyone loves their local gear shop or their local running store. And here in Bellingham, we've got a couple of great running stores. How do you not, I dunno, abandon them. How do you still support them while building out this e-commerce, these tools and flushing out your presence there while not leaving the local running shop in the dust?", "token_estimate": 304, "speakers": ["Linda Balfour", "Speaker 3", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "18:32", "end_timestamp": "19:09", "podcast_index": 36, "episode_name": "Ep 36: Linda Balfour | E-commerce Pivot | Superfee‪t‬", "slug": "ep-36-linda-balfour-e-commerce-pivot-superfeet", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-36-linda-balfour-e-commerce-pivot-superfeet", "guest_name": "Linda Balfour", "guest_title": "Superfeet", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"COVID brought us closer together and made us look at how we can do things more efficiently.\"", "podcast_length": "44:58"}, {"id": "4b5f16d7-70df-4843-81c7-dbc91fe4ad30", "episode_id": "173150f4-bcd8-4775-a2c4-504412f06ad0", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4847", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Linda Balfour (19:40): Yeah, so I think this has been the cool thing about a company the size of Super FETs is that everybody works together and we have a mantra internally, one team, and we really live that. So while the marketing team is really focused on building great tools and digital media that's driving people to the right spots and how we merchandise online both on superfeet.com and then the Giants like Amazon, we also have our field services team that is starting to actually do, they would go into store at Fair and Runners or Bellingham Bay Runners or something like that. They would go into the store and do a visual audit of what's there and help the retailer merchandise if it makes sense for them or if they need to move things around. Well, our field services team is now doing that for our specialty retailers websites. Oh, cool. So how cool is that they're now, instead of going, they're treating the online retail environment almost the same way that they would the physical retail environment. So they can say, Hey, we noticed that you don't have insoles in your dropdown menu, or you have to navigate three steps in to get there. What do you think about adding it into your navigation? We saw when we did that with one of our retailers that their insole sales went up 30% just by adding insoles in place that people could see it.\n\nSpeaker 3 (21:03): Interesting,\n\nLinda Balfour (21:05): Simple things that we think of as marketers, but if you're a retailer, you may not also, or that kind of cross merchandising, but specialty retail, they just haven't had that length of expertise in online yet. So we're trying to help however we can with that.\n\nCole Heilborn (21:21): Yeah, no, that's cool. So how are you guys thinking about, I guess, the e-commerce as a whole? Because I know a lot of people have varying opinions. How does Superfeet view it? And if you could, I dunno, leave your own little presence or your own brand value in the world of e-commerce, what would that be?", "token_estimate": 501, "speakers": ["Linda Balfour", "Speaker 3", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "19:40", "end_timestamp": "21:21", "podcast_index": 36, "episode_name": "Ep 36: Linda Balfour | E-commerce Pivot | Superfee‪t‬", "slug": "ep-36-linda-balfour-e-commerce-pivot-superfeet", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-36-linda-balfour-e-commerce-pivot-superfeet", "guest_name": "Linda Balfour", "guest_title": "Superfeet", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"COVID brought us closer together and made us look at how we can do things more efficiently.\"", "podcast_length": "44:58"}, {"id": "26750511-7ce8-4c06-8880-d77037cda6c0", "episode_id": "173150f4-bcd8-4775-a2c4-504412f06ad0", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4847", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Linda Balfour (21:46): We are really, we're taking a pretty holistic look on our online business. We're looking at the way that Superfeet works with the major marketplaces, with specialty retail, and doing a lot of work to understand who's shopping where and why, so that we can make sure that they have the right tools when they get to that spot. Because going to a running store website for a different reason potentially than you'd go to super feed.com and you're shopping differently on Amazon than you would at their running store website. So really understanding how shoppers are using each of those sites and how to best serve them on those sites is really important to us. But then also, I mean holistically, honestly, I probably shouldn't say this, but I don't really care if people shop on superfeet.com or if they shop on your specialty outdoor store.com, right? In fact, I would probably prefer 'em to shop on specialty outdoor.com because we need those retailers to really help curate and build this outdoor experience for us. (22:57): So I think we're looking at this world of how can we be the next generation of retail? How can we act like a digitally native brand even though we're 40 plus years old and bring everyone along with us, or at least support 'em in their quest to do the same thing? Because outdoor is changing, outdoor participants are changing, the way that people shop for outdoor is changing. And so how do we be as innovative as we were when we launched insoles? Now, how do we relaunch insoles online? Do we make buying them easy no matter where you are? How do we make it real easy to find no matter where you're shopping?\n\nCole Heilborn (23:40): Is that challenging knowing that you've got this legacy that you're working with and the history of the company as you're trying to make this next change?", "token_estimate": 460, "speakers": ["Linda Balfour", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "21:46", "end_timestamp": "23:40", "podcast_index": 36, "episode_name": "Ep 36: Linda Balfour | E-commerce Pivot | Superfee‪t‬", "slug": "ep-36-linda-balfour-e-commerce-pivot-superfeet", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-36-linda-balfour-e-commerce-pivot-superfeet", "guest_name": "Linda Balfour", "guest_title": "Superfeet", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"COVID brought us closer together and made us look at how we can do things more efficiently.\"", "podcast_length": "44:58"}, {"id": "c3703118-f5a8-45e6-b9b4-196fcaf6694e", "episode_id": "173150f4-bcd8-4775-a2c4-504412f06ad0", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4847", "chunk_index": 12, "content": "Linda Balfour (23:52): I think it's just really fun. It's definitely, sometimes you have to stop and unthink the things that you already know. (24:03): I think really a challenge for us to get to the point of how do we compare Superfeet Blue versus the run series versus a casual product? And we just knew too much about it. We were trying to make it, we were trying to tell everybody everything about it. And so we just had to take a couple steps back and simplify it. And then really thinking, when Cole's looking for insoles, what is it that you want? You mean you probably just want a more comfortable shoe, so how do we get you to the most comfortable combination of products versus telling you everything about that insole and how do we do it quickly and in a way that you're like, okay, I get it.\n\nCole Heilborn (24:49): Oh, interesting. Are there two or three skills that you've had to really put to the test during this shift, again, as a marketer, and what have you thought about as you've been asking yourselves these questions?", "token_estimate": 260, "speakers": ["Linda Balfour", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "23:52", "end_timestamp": "24:49", "podcast_index": 36, "episode_name": "Ep 36: Linda Balfour | E-commerce Pivot | Superfee‪t‬", "slug": "ep-36-linda-balfour-e-commerce-pivot-superfeet", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-36-linda-balfour-e-commerce-pivot-superfeet", "guest_name": "Linda Balfour", "guest_title": "Superfeet", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"COVID brought us closer together and made us look at how we can do things more efficiently.\"", "podcast_length": "44:58"}, {"id": "ba29cc36-a790-41ac-b8f8-74e406cb8665", "episode_id": "173150f4-bcd8-4775-a2c4-504412f06ad0", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4847", "chunk_index": 13, "content": "Linda Balfour (25:03): I think that being a really interesting brand has always relied on very good storytelling. And I think we're just needing to find new ways to tell stories, (25:20): And not only new ways to tell stories, but new ways to invite people into those and to help them see themselves there. And I think social media is such a great way to do that, and it's always changing. And our social media manager is amazing and she knows everything about it, but how do we make sure that the work that she's doing translates to the work that our retail marketing manager is doing, who's also doing our online audits? How do those two pieces work together? So I think while being a well orchestrated, really solid marketing team has always included being a good storyteller and working well as a team, we've just had to make kind of subtle shifts in the way that we're doing that in order to really do that well in an online environment. And so I think it's a skill. It's just strengthening your skills. It's kind of cross training a little bit, I guess. (26:10): So that's one thing. I think the other thing that has been, I don't know that it's a skill, but I mentioned our retailers a couple of times, and retail has always been really, really important to us, and relationships are really, really important to us. And so I think we've had to be better at working with our retailers, not only in that online environment, and how do we give them the tools that they need to be to be this destination really interesting, rich shopping experience when somebody is in, but then how do we get people also to embrace this idea that we are after that the world is after, which is that we also need to be equally strong online. And I think COVID definitely sped that up because retailers who never needed a website before all have 'em now. And so they're there and we're there for them. But that was definitely a skill or something, I would just say maybe an area that there was a little bit of a gap that we needed to fill.\n\nCole Heilborn (27:13): Yeah, yeah. What's been the biggest challenge, or has there been a big challenge for you during this change?", "token_estimate": 540, "speakers": ["Linda Balfour", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "25:03", "end_timestamp": "27:13", "podcast_index": 36, "episode_name": "Ep 36: Linda Balfour | E-commerce Pivot | Superfee‪t‬", "slug": "ep-36-linda-balfour-e-commerce-pivot-superfeet", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-36-linda-balfour-e-commerce-pivot-superfeet", "guest_name": "Linda Balfour", "guest_title": "Superfeet", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"COVID brought us closer together and made us look at how we can do things more efficiently.\"", "podcast_length": "44:58"}, {"id": "7f2247b4-879a-4d6e-94a8-106c3cbf016c", "episode_id": "173150f4-bcd8-4775-a2c4-504412f06ad0", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4847", "chunk_index": 14, "content": "Linda Balfour (27:27): I think the challenges that I feel right now probably have more to do with the world at large than with what's going on at work. And I feel super grateful for that. Our team in these really trying times has come together and has operated on a level beyond what they ever worked before. (27:54): And I think I feel super grateful to our IT team that the day that we decided to work from home, everybody was able to walk out of the building that day with their computer equipment plug in, and on Monday morning everybody was back up and working. Nothing happened. So that was pretty cool. And then it's just now reconciling as a brand. I think the hardest thing is where is our voice in current events right now? How do we feel about the social unrest that's going on? How vocal are we willing to be about it? How do we feel about the environmental decisions that our president is making for us right now? And how do we use the influence that we have as a company or as part of a bigger industry to help see the outcomes that we want through? And really a lot of it is much more around us finding our feet and the competence in our voice and using it in a way that we feel like is really good for our employees, good for our consumers, and good for the world.\n\nCole Heilborn (28:57): Wow. Well, that's another topic right there, and I had love to go down that route, but we're running out of time. Maybe we'll have to follow up in the new year and do something. But Linda, if people want to follow along with the work that either you're doing or the team is doing, where can people go to get connected and see all the latest and greatest from Superfeet?\n\nLinda Balfour (29:20): Yeah. So Super Feed is just superfeet.com real easy. Our social handles are the same. So we're at superfeet and yeah, follow us. Join us. We'd love to have you\n\nCole Heilborn (29:31): Find you guys on the trails somewhere.", "token_estimate": 481, "speakers": ["Linda Balfour", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "27:27", "end_timestamp": "29:31", "podcast_index": 36, "episode_name": "Ep 36: Linda Balfour | E-commerce Pivot | Superfee‪t‬", "slug": "ep-36-linda-balfour-e-commerce-pivot-superfeet", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-36-linda-balfour-e-commerce-pivot-superfeet", "guest_name": "Linda Balfour", "guest_title": "Superfeet", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"COVID brought us closer together and made us look at how we can do things more efficiently.\"", "podcast_length": "44:58"}, {"id": "9c51b838-52b3-4ecc-a2d9-e7656d34e1e0", "episode_id": "173150f4-bcd8-4775-a2c4-504412f06ad0", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4847", "chunk_index": 15, "content": "Linda Balfour (29:33): Yeah, find us on the trails. Gosh, find us on the trails across the country. We have our sales rep course is actually all in-house sales reps. They're all across the country or in Europe and Canada. So look for Superfeet jerseys on people and tech shirts and headbands and ask 'em about superheat, for sure.\n\nCole Heilborn (29:52): Alright, well, Linda, thank you so much. Have a good rest of your day, and we'll chat later. Thanks, you too. All right, bye. Thanks again for listening to this episode. If you enjoyed it, please consider sharing it with a friend and leaving us a review on Apple. Also, remember to check out Darby Communications. If you're looking for a great PR and marketing team, go ahead and find 'em at darby communications.com.", "token_estimate": 193, "speakers": ["Linda Balfour", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "29:33", "end_timestamp": "29:52", "podcast_index": 36, "episode_name": "Ep 36: Linda Balfour | E-commerce Pivot | Superfee‪t‬", "slug": "ep-36-linda-balfour-e-commerce-pivot-superfeet", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-36-linda-balfour-e-commerce-pivot-superfeet", "guest_name": "Linda Balfour", "guest_title": "Superfeet", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"COVID brought us closer together and made us look at how we can do things more efficiently.\"", "podcast_length": "44:58"}, {"id": "2307e38e-5599-49e3-9b90-c4b9edbbcb60", "episode_id": "a928ece0-f5c2-4649-8536-4dbd83497c09", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4848", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:02): Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Hilborn and I'll be your host today. This podcast dives deep with leaders of the outdoor marketing industry as they discuss the gritty details of their work, personal challenges and latest adventures, whether you're standing in the lift line, shuttling to fresh loam, or cracking a local beer. I hope you enjoyed today's episode. This is the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. This episode is produced by Portside Productions, an outdoor, commercial, and film production company based in the Pacific Northwest. Before we jump into today's episode, I wanted to talk about Darby Communications. They're our presenting sponsor for this episode. I've gotten to know them over the last few months as they've helped me with some PR and marketing for this podcast. The Darby Comm team has designed their process around my needs, combining a people first mindset with an analytical data-driven approach. Today's business world moves fast, but the Darby Comm team has taken the time to truly get to know me and understand my goals. They've been a great partner to collaborate with, and I'm really impressed with their well-rounded approach that generate real results that actually help bring this podcast to more years. If you're in the market for a great PR team in the outdoor wellness and fitness industry, go ahead and check 'em out@darbycommunications.com. (01:19): Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Hilborn. Today I'm sitting down with Jen Rei, the CEO of Coalition Snow. Jen, how are you?\n\nJen Gurecki (01:27): I'm doing well, thank you. How are you?\n\nCole Heilborn (01:29): I'm doing fantastic. Super excited to be chatting with you today on this nice sunny morning up here in the Pacific Northwest. Where are you calling in from today?\n\nJen Gurecki (01:38): I am in Reno, Nevada.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:40): Okay, right on. So for folks that don't know, give us a little bit of background on Coalition Snow. Who are you? What are you guys up to?", "token_estimate": 513, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Jen Gurecki"], "start_timestamp": "00:02", "end_timestamp": "01:40", "podcast_index": 37, "episode_name": "Ep 37: Jen Gurecki | Snow for All | Coalition Sno‪w‬", "slug": "ep-37-jen-gurecki-snow-for-all-coalition-snow", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-37-jen-gurecki-snow-for-all-coalition-snow", "guest_name": "Jen Gurecki", "guest_title": "CEO, Coalition Snow", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"There's not a team of marketing professionals sitting around a table trying to come up with a campaign, or what should we say. Everything you see and read and hear is 100% real. Real talk, from us, it's how we feel.\"", "podcast_length": "44:59"}, {"id": "9826ad37-40cd-4a11-b493-c8cd83d0f4be", "episode_id": "a928ece0-f5c2-4649-8536-4dbd83497c09", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4848", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Jen Gurecki (01:50): Yep. So Coalition Snow is a woman owned and operated ski and snowboard company. So we actually make skis and snowboards. Sometimes I have to explain that multiple times because people think we make apparel or we're a community group. And while we do a lot of things, including both of those, we started off seven years ago deciding that we didn't want to ski and ride on skis that were kind of the whole shrinking and pink it thing. We weren't really satisfied with what was available to us. We just kind of waited around to see if anything better would come out on the market, and it kind of never did. So we just took things into our own hands and started designing the skis and the snowboards that we wanted to ski and ride on, and that's how Coalition Snow was started.\n\nCole Heilborn (02:41): Beautiful. And how long have you been around?\n\nJen Gurecki (02:43): We're heading into our seventh season right now.\n\nWow. Wow. Congratulations. (02:48): That's cool. So you kind of alluded to, I think what we're going to chat about today. What I'm excited to chat about is you guys have, well, part of the thing we're going to chat about is you guys have tons of platforms that you guys tell stories with, connect with your audiences with, and I'm excited to kind of dive into that a little bit deeper and talk about what those platforms are and how does that wrap up into Coalition Snow's mission, but maybe kind of break those down. You guys have a podcast, you have a quarterly magazine. What are those, and is there anything else there that I'm missing that you guys have built for the company?", "token_estimate": 405, "speakers": ["Jen Gurecki", "Cole Heilborn", "Wow. Wow. Congratulations."], "start_timestamp": "01:50", "end_timestamp": "02:48", "podcast_index": 37, "episode_name": "Ep 37: Jen Gurecki | Snow for All | Coalition Sno‪w‬", "slug": "ep-37-jen-gurecki-snow-for-all-coalition-snow", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-37-jen-gurecki-snow-for-all-coalition-snow", "guest_name": "Jen Gurecki", "guest_title": "CEO, Coalition Snow", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"There's not a team of marketing professionals sitting around a table trying to come up with a campaign, or what should we say. Everything you see and read and hear is 100% real. Real talk, from us, it's how we feel.\"", "podcast_length": "44:59"}, {"id": "074d255a-394c-4e79-b364-f6769690f2f8", "episode_id": "a928ece0-f5c2-4649-8536-4dbd83497c09", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4848", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Jen Gurecki (03:24): Yeah, so we have a podcast called Juicy Bits. It's pretty irreverent and inappropriate and might have an R rating on iTunes. Just saying, that's where myself and one of our ambassadors, Jillian, just we talk about anything we want to and we also interview people and it's pretty fun. So that's Juicy Bits. In December of 2018, we launched sisu Magazine, which is a quarterly print magazine whose mission is to uncover the untold stories of the outdoors. So we are working on issue eight right now and that we are really embracing an old school format there with print. We do have a web present, but we're not what we're really going for. We want to just kind of slow things down there. And then something that we'll be launching shortly toward the end of October is we will have a few community groups. So we're actually kind of moving off Facebook for all the reasons why you would want to move off Facebook, and we're launching community groups. We have one that'll be specifically for Bipoc community, which is created and run by a few of our Bipoc ambassadors. And then we'll have one that's really for co-conspirators and people who want to see Snow sports become a more inclusive and welcoming place. So we'll be launching that shortly.\n\nCole Heilborn (04:57): Okay, right on. You said something that's interesting. You created the magazine because you wanted to tell stories that stories that weren't being told. And I feel like that's interesting because a lot of people say that these days, but looking through your magazine, I think it's very obvious that you guys are telling stories that no one else is telling.\n\nJen Gurecki (05:17): Yeah.\n\nCole Heilborn (05:20): So how does all of these different platforms, these different community groups, how does this all kind of get wrapped up into Coalition Snow and what mission you guys are on?", "token_estimate": 468, "speakers": ["Jen Gurecki", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "03:24", "end_timestamp": "05:20", "podcast_index": 37, "episode_name": "Ep 37: Jen Gurecki | Snow for All | Coalition Sno‪w‬", "slug": "ep-37-jen-gurecki-snow-for-all-coalition-snow", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-37-jen-gurecki-snow-for-all-coalition-snow", "guest_name": "Jen Gurecki", "guest_title": "CEO, Coalition Snow", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"There's not a team of marketing professionals sitting around a table trying to come up with a campaign, or what should we say. Everything you see and read and hear is 100% real. Real talk, from us, it's how we feel.\"", "podcast_length": "44:59"}, {"id": "bda488ca-4458-42cf-b83d-23371d681e72", "episode_id": "a928ece0-f5c2-4649-8536-4dbd83497c09", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4848", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Jen Gurecki (05:30): Yeah, well, definitely it's been a process and been an evolution of the brand. I think we got to a point where we realized that we were sort of transitioning from to a platform, so people were showing up and wanting to be a part of what we were building regardless of whether or not they were skiers or snowboarders, regardless of whether or not they skied or rode on our product. So we recognized that and we also began to recognize the responsibility that we have as essentially privileged white women in a privileged sport that we created this platform, and if we started our company based on a mission of inclusivity and diversity in snow sports, we really needed to walk that talk. And so looking at all the different platforms that, all the opportunities that were there for us to essentially open up this platform to other people and to stories that sort of challenged a dominant narrative in snow sports became really critical in terms of us upholding our values and also being responsive to our community as well.\n\nCole Heilborn (06:51): So I feel like people, when they look at what you're doing, they would look at you guys and say, oh man, I love what they're doing. I resonate with Coalition Snow and their mission, and some people would describe that as thought leadership in marketing. Is that something that you guys were aware of that you were creating, or is it just kind of happened naturally and you've realized, oh, we're at this place now, where people kind of look to us for presenting stories and offering guidance on critical issues that we believe in?", "token_estimate": 400, "speakers": ["Jen Gurecki", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "05:30", "end_timestamp": "06:51", "podcast_index": 37, "episode_name": "Ep 37: Jen Gurecki | Snow for All | Coalition Sno‪w‬", "slug": "ep-37-jen-gurecki-snow-for-all-coalition-snow", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-37-jen-gurecki-snow-for-all-coalition-snow", "guest_name": "Jen Gurecki", "guest_title": "CEO, Coalition Snow", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"There's not a team of marketing professionals sitting around a table trying to come up with a campaign, or what should we say. Everything you see and read and hear is 100% real. Real talk, from us, it's how we feel.\"", "podcast_length": "44:59"}, {"id": "26eefcb6-79ff-451a-985f-6b1f6689c5c3", "episode_id": "a928ece0-f5c2-4649-8536-4dbd83497c09", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4848", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Jen Gurecki (07:23): Yeah, I would say when we started Coalition Snow, we definitely, I did not consider myself a thought leader in the industry. We were challenging the industry absolutely and saying, let's take pause here and really understand who's in the margins of snow sports. And we weren't thought leaders when we first started. I think that's something that happens over time. And I would say absolutely when we recognize that we were sort of in that position, certainly responded to it. And everything that we do is incredibly thoughtful might, if you go to our Instagram, you might be like, well, why are they posting that and what's this about? But it's strategic, it's thoughtful, and it all feeds into the greater mission and values of the company. But yeah, it's been a process, I'd say.\n\nCole Heilborn (08:26): Yeah. Could you kind of break down that process a little bit more? You guys started out just wanting to challenge the status quo, and I guess you started doing that day one. What has that journey looked like to where you are now?", "token_estimate": 264, "speakers": ["Jen Gurecki", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "07:23", "end_timestamp": "08:26", "podcast_index": 37, "episode_name": "Ep 37: Jen Gurecki | Snow for All | Coalition Sno‪w‬", "slug": "ep-37-jen-gurecki-snow-for-all-coalition-snow", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-37-jen-gurecki-snow-for-all-coalition-snow", "guest_name": "Jen Gurecki", "guest_title": "CEO, Coalition Snow", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"There's not a team of marketing professionals sitting around a table trying to come up with a campaign, or what should we say. Everything you see and read and hear is 100% real. Real talk, from us, it's how we feel.\"", "podcast_length": "44:59"}, {"id": "9c79126e-32f5-489e-8c45-0c30f2df8b7f", "episode_id": "a928ece0-f5c2-4649-8536-4dbd83497c09", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4848", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Jen Gurecki (08:41): I would say at the beginning, it felt like a fight. It felt like we really had to push in a big way for any recognition people. I mean, snow sports with a lot of outdoors, it's just like, are you a cool kid or not? Do you know the right people? Do you have who holds the keys? Do you know that person who holds the keys? Do you? And we didn't come from the industry. We are just women who like to ski and ride, and we've been doing it for decades. And so we weren't industry insiders. Nobody knew who we were. And so when we launched, there was certainly disbelief, I would say. People were like, who cares? Who are they? Also, it's just a lot of not believing that we were capable of designing skis and snowboards and creating a product that was performance driven and outstanding. (09:46): We don't look like your typical ski bum. We don't look like your typical ski manufacturer or brand, and we never have. So I think at the beginning, it really felt like we had to pull out all the gorilla marketing tactics that we could. We had no budget, we had no connections, and we just had to really think about what was our competitive market advantage. And for us, it essentially was and still is, we're the only feminist ski company. We are owned by women. We're run by a hundred percent of what we do is women focused, women driven. And so we just really, that's what we know. So we went with it. And there's never been, I think what we've seen over the years is another thing that makes us different is that there's not a team of marketing professionals sitting around a table trying to come up with a campaign or what should we say? (10:42): Everything you see and read and hear is just a hundred percent real, real talk from us. It's how we feel. It's how we perceive the world. And I think that that's what really resonates with people is that authenticity over the years, because we have remained consistent in the way in which we show up in this industry and the world, and because we can continue to be responsive to our community, I would say that now in year seven, it feels more like a celebration than a fight, and it's still certainly difficult. I mean, I'm the dumbest, smartest person you've ever met to start a hard goods business in a seasonal industry that's male dominated. I mean, I don't know what I was thinking, but it does, I think that we've earned the respect and the recognition that does come with so many years of hard work and failing and succeeding and just picking ourselves up and just deciding to do this every day and really working hard to show up for people. So yeah.", "token_estimate": 656, "speakers": ["Jen Gurecki"], "start_timestamp": "08:41", "end_timestamp": "08:41", "podcast_index": 37, "episode_name": "Ep 37: Jen Gurecki | Snow for All | Coalition Sno‪w‬", "slug": "ep-37-jen-gurecki-snow-for-all-coalition-snow", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-37-jen-gurecki-snow-for-all-coalition-snow", "guest_name": "Jen Gurecki", "guest_title": "CEO, Coalition Snow", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"There's not a team of marketing professionals sitting around a table trying to come up with a campaign, or what should we say. Everything you see and read and hear is 100% real. Real talk, from us, it's how we feel.\"", "podcast_length": "44:59"}, {"id": "b9f8d184-8821-40b7-ab42-2621d89d426a", "episode_id": "a928ece0-f5c2-4649-8536-4dbd83497c09", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4848", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Cole Heilborn (11:56): What type of response have you gotten from your audience? How did they feel when you guys entered the space and gave themselves a voice where they could connect and just have conversations? What was that like?\n\nJen Gurecki (12:11): I think that it's important to acknowledge that not all women are the same, and not all women appreciate us. And we can see that right now happening politically where not all women represent all women. You can't put us in a box. So I would say that when we first started, there were heaps of women who were like, wow, this is amazing. Finally, somebody's here for us and we're being listened to and we're understood. And then there were other women who were like, just shut it up. Just shut it. Get out of here. There's not a problem. Everything's fine. Why are you so loud? Forget about it. Right. And that continues to be the case. We are not for everyone. We talk about things that some people do not like and they don't like the way that we talk about it. And that's okay For us, we have decided as a company that as long as we're being honest and true to ourselves and staying in line with our values, then we're not here to make everyone happy. We're not here to serve all women, but we certainly know what it is that we stand for and where we're willing to toe the line on that.\n\nCole Heilborn (13:23): What type of backlash have you received? Did that get in the way or did that deter you as you guys were first starting out?", "token_estimate": 372, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Jen Gurecki"], "start_timestamp": "11:56", "end_timestamp": "13:23", "podcast_index": 37, "episode_name": "Ep 37: Jen Gurecki | Snow for All | Coalition Sno‪w‬", "slug": "ep-37-jen-gurecki-snow-for-all-coalition-snow", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-37-jen-gurecki-snow-for-all-coalition-snow", "guest_name": "Jen Gurecki", "guest_title": "CEO, Coalition Snow", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"There's not a team of marketing professionals sitting around a table trying to come up with a campaign, or what should we say. Everything you see and read and hear is 100% real. Real talk, from us, it's how we feel.\"", "podcast_length": "44:59"}, {"id": "63a49838-bf59-4d40-8034-46d1f617aa51", "episode_id": "a928ece0-f5c2-4649-8536-4dbd83497c09", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4848", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Jen Gurecki (13:30): No, I mean, I just would say it's like your typical backlash that any strong, outspoken women get. It's not unique to snow sports. This is just life. And I think that that's probably why it didn't deter us as I was like, oh, just another day. Showing up in this world as a woman, where I decided to use my voice and stand up for myself, and that makes other people not feel good about themselves. I can't be responsible for people's fragility, their insecurity, their unwillingness to accept change. There's a lot of different reasons why, or just they just don't like us. I mean, there's a million reasons why, but it wasn't new. It's not like I've lived a life of everyone giving me a pat on the back and telling me that everything I do is fantastic. You deal with things as a woman, and I'm a white woman, so even what I deal with is far less than what women of color have to manage. But so no, it did not deter me, I would say, actually, I was just like, oh yeah, watch this. Here we go.\n\nCole Heilborn (14:34): And seven years later.\n\nJen Gurecki (14:35): Yeah, exactly.\n\nCole Heilborn (14:38): So describe your own journey becoming a thought leader. What has that been like for you and what have you learned and what's the responsibility that's come with that position?", "token_estimate": 324, "speakers": ["Jen Gurecki", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "13:30", "end_timestamp": "14:38", "podcast_index": 37, "episode_name": "Ep 37: Jen Gurecki | Snow for All | Coalition Sno‪w‬", "slug": "ep-37-jen-gurecki-snow-for-all-coalition-snow", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-37-jen-gurecki-snow-for-all-coalition-snow", "guest_name": "Jen Gurecki", "guest_title": "CEO, Coalition Snow", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"There's not a team of marketing professionals sitting around a table trying to come up with a campaign, or what should we say. Everything you see and read and hear is 100% real. Real talk, from us, it's how we feel.\"", "podcast_length": "44:59"}, {"id": "eeb48d64-c569-42fa-ab4d-5b0828373990", "episode_id": "a928ece0-f5c2-4649-8536-4dbd83497c09", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4848", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Jen Gurecki (14:46): Well, I think that one thing that's important to acknowledge here is that I had a long career and academic experience prior to starting coalition snow. So I have a master's degree in non-formal education and community development. I dropped out of a PhD right before I was getting ready to write my dissertation, and I spent years studying feminist theory, critical race theory, economics, sustainability. So there's a lot of theory bound in what we put out as a company, and I think that that theory combined with experiences is what has allowed for the thought leadership to naturally occur. I think also prior to coalition, I ran wilderness programming at the University of California. I owned a whitewater rafting company. I've started a social enterprise in East Africa. I've done a lot of things. And so I don't know if I would be as prepared to speak to the things that I speak to now if it wasn't for those years and years of research in years of experience. And that just kind of goes back to what I said earlier, that this isn't a group of people sitting around a table trying to figure out what to\n\nCole Heilborn (16:15): Say.\n\nJen Gurecki (16:17): It just comes out of my head and either out of my mouth or onto a piece of paper and it gets put out there. I feel really fortunate that that is a skill that I have and that I've cultivated for two decades now.\n\nCole Heilborn (16:32): With that though, why do you think we're seeing such a, I dunno, we're seeing such a push for thought leadership in marketing across all industries. I feel like specifically a lot in the outdoors with that though, there's a lot of companies or individuals I think, who are real, who are authentic, and then there's those who maybe are just out there not backing up what they're saying and are just trying to reach that level of credibility without the credibility. I guess. What's your take on what we're seeing today in the marketing world when it comes to thought leadership?", "token_estimate": 499, "speakers": ["Jen Gurecki", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "14:46", "end_timestamp": "16:32", "podcast_index": 37, "episode_name": "Ep 37: Jen Gurecki | Snow for All | Coalition Sno‪w‬", "slug": "ep-37-jen-gurecki-snow-for-all-coalition-snow", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-37-jen-gurecki-snow-for-all-coalition-snow", "guest_name": "Jen Gurecki", "guest_title": "CEO, Coalition Snow", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"There's not a team of marketing professionals sitting around a table trying to come up with a campaign, or what should we say. Everything you see and read and hear is 100% real. Real talk, from us, it's how we feel.\"", "podcast_length": "44:59"}, {"id": "5a2ac87a-3306-44a2-aeae-273978038035", "episode_id": "a928ece0-f5c2-4649-8536-4dbd83497c09", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4848", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Jen Gurecki (17:09): Well, we have changing demographics of consumers, and consumers want. They actually want to see a brand take on the persona of human beings, and they actually want to know who those human beings are, and they want to know what they believe in and what they do. And so this is fundamentally different. If you look at generations, older generations don't necessarily have affinity for brands. Younger generations do, and younger generations also, I think just based on what they've dealt with in the few years they've spent on this planet is that they really look toward a lot of different avenues for change to occur. And they want to see change, whether it's about healthcare or universal or education, the economy, they weren't born in a good time in this country. And so they really want to see that companies are doing something to make the world a better place. (18:20): So what we're seeing in marketing is being driven by the consumer, and so you have to be responsive. With that said, what I'll say is that what marketers or anybody in business should take away from this is that this isn't something that you can just Google one day and write about, or this is lifelong work. Understanding what these issues are and making change is a daily commitment. It's not something that you just roll out in a campaign and then you walk away from it. And consumers are going to be savvy to that, and they will do their homework to see who is what behind the messages. So this is where hiring the right people who have the experiences, the education, the training to be able to tell real stories is really important. And what's beautiful about it is that in and of itself could diversify the workforce in the outdoor industry because you cannot have a 40-year-old, cisgendered, straight white man tell the story of a 25-year-old black queer woman. It is impossible. So rather than try to tell it, get out of the way and let those people come in and tell their own stories and and help you do your job better. And I think that that's what consumers are going to respond to.", "token_estimate": 523, "speakers": ["Jen Gurecki"], "start_timestamp": "17:09", "end_timestamp": "17:09", "podcast_index": 37, "episode_name": "Ep 37: Jen Gurecki | Snow for All | Coalition Sno‪w‬", "slug": "ep-37-jen-gurecki-snow-for-all-coalition-snow", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-37-jen-gurecki-snow-for-all-coalition-snow", "guest_name": "Jen Gurecki", "guest_title": "CEO, Coalition Snow", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"There's not a team of marketing professionals sitting around a table trying to come up with a campaign, or what should we say. Everything you see and read and hear is 100% real. Real talk, from us, it's how we feel.\"", "podcast_length": "44:59"}, {"id": "d4c8a1a3-9fe8-4f1e-a079-640a7710039f", "episode_id": "a928ece0-f5c2-4649-8536-4dbd83497c09", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4848", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Cole Heilborn (19:47): Yeah, I think it's no surprise that consumers are the smartest they've ever been today, and we have the tools to look into these issues. And who's saying what? Why are they saying what? Yeah, I think it's interesting and it's a lot more complicated than just coming up with some fancy initiative and then rolling it out. And I think what I like about what you guys are doing is that you've been doing this kind of quietly personally. You've been doing this quietly and working on stuff for the last many years, and now you just have built this platform where you guys are able to speak about\n\nJen Gurecki (20:23): This.\n\nCole Heilborn (20:25): What are some other brands out there that you respect and who are doing, and I guess what you would describe good work?", "token_estimate": 197, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Jen Gurecki"], "start_timestamp": "19:47", "end_timestamp": "20:25", "podcast_index": 37, "episode_name": "Ep 37: Jen Gurecki | Snow for All | Coalition Sno‪w‬", "slug": "ep-37-jen-gurecki-snow-for-all-coalition-snow", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-37-jen-gurecki-snow-for-all-coalition-snow", "guest_name": "Jen Gurecki", "guest_title": "CEO, Coalition Snow", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"There's not a team of marketing professionals sitting around a table trying to come up with a campaign, or what should we say. Everything you see and read and hear is 100% real. Real talk, from us, it's how we feel.\"", "podcast_length": "44:59"}, {"id": "c1c0b158-5660-4d16-bffc-27ba6a97dbd6", "episode_id": "a928ece0-f5c2-4649-8536-4dbd83497c09", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4848", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Jen Gurecki (20:33): Yeah, I think one obvious one would be Patagonia, the work that they've done around the environment, around voting. Just recently it was disclosed that they put a little secret hidden message on some of their tags. They have some secret code on their website. When Rose decided to donate her entire 10 million tax refund she got from Trump, that's big. So Patagonia as a privately held company has been able to do some, I think, pretty spectacular work. Obviously they have things to work on, and I think they're aware of it and they're speaking to that, but they haven't really been afraid to talk about the environment and to talk about voting. And that message does resonate to some extent pretty well with their target demographic. I think a lot of brands will be challenged the more that they talk about race and they talk about gender, because at the end of the day, a lot of people who are consuming these messages from the outdoor industry are cisgendered straight white men. (21:45): So I think there's some challenges there, but I think Patagonia does a really good job. There's a number of brands outside of the outdoor industry that I look toward because I actually don't see, I don't see the level, I don't necessarily see what I want to see in the industry. I do think that the outdoor industry is very conservative. I think that some people recognize that and other people also don't because they don't realize how their message is really catered to white men still. So I tend to look like Wild Fang would be a brand that I look at. So they're women owned out of Portland apparel and accessories, but have always been very outspoken. I'm very supportive of the L-G-B-T-Q-I-A community. One of their founders identifies in that community, so they've always sent it pretty hard. So I follow them. And then there's just a lot of, I'd say smaller indie brands popping up in the sort of lifestyle, health and wellness space that I spend more time looking at because they are talking about the things and saying things in the way that resonates with me. And I think that that's indicative of the spectrum of interests of women in the outdoors.", "token_estimate": 544, "speakers": ["Jen Gurecki"], "start_timestamp": "20:33", "end_timestamp": "20:33", "podcast_index": 37, "episode_name": "Ep 37: Jen Gurecki | Snow for All | Coalition Sno‪w‬", "slug": "ep-37-jen-gurecki-snow-for-all-coalition-snow", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-37-jen-gurecki-snow-for-all-coalition-snow", "guest_name": "Jen Gurecki", "guest_title": "CEO, Coalition Snow", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"There's not a team of marketing professionals sitting around a table trying to come up with a campaign, or what should we say. Everything you see and read and hear is 100% real. Real talk, from us, it's how we feel.\"", "podcast_length": "44:59"}, {"id": "a6359872-b1c6-4761-a842-4fa6518965cd", "episode_id": "a928ece0-f5c2-4649-8536-4dbd83497c09", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4848", "chunk_index": 12, "content": "Cole Heilborn (23:19): Yeah. Okay. Very cool. Yeah, it's always interesting to hear what are some other brands out there that are doing really good work? And there's so many that I feel like we haven't heard of who are just plugging away doing their thing. For you, as you look forward for the future of Coalition Snow, what do you hope for the future of the company? And what are some things that you guys look at and you think, oh, we could do a better job at X?\n\nJen Gurecki (23:48): Yeah. Well, I would hope that I can be replaced soon. I would love nothing then to be able to pass the torch on to somebody who can do a better job than me. So the future of coalition is number one, sticking around still being here, which let's be honest, year to year, when you're a small brand in snow sports, you kind of really, you're like, oh, hope we make it to next year. So I would like for us to stick around, and I would like to be able to step aside and let someone else come in and take the company to the next place where it needs to go. And I also think that what we have been attempting to do over the last few years, and I hope that we can continue to do it better, is just really demonstrate that when we talk about being here for women, that we mean all women, that we mean trans women, we mean black women, we mean queer women, even to be quite honest, we could do a better job of acknowledging that they're non-binary individuals who our values resonate, but we are not necessarily showing up in the way that we could. (25:12): And I think that's where having other people step into leadership positions in the company would be a really good thing. But I'd like us to be able to do that. And part of sticking around part of a transition in leadership and part of really ensuring that we're staying true to our values is all of that leads to substantial change in the snow sports industry because the industry is privileged. It can be very toxic. And at the end of the day, the outdoors are for everyone, and skiing and snowboarding is super fun, and everyone deserves to be out there and doing it and enjoy it. And that can look different for anyone or for everyone. So I would just like to see that happen.", "token_estimate": 554, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Jen Gurecki"], "start_timestamp": "23:19", "end_timestamp": "23:48", "podcast_index": 37, "episode_name": "Ep 37: Jen Gurecki | Snow for All | Coalition Sno‪w‬", "slug": "ep-37-jen-gurecki-snow-for-all-coalition-snow", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-37-jen-gurecki-snow-for-all-coalition-snow", "guest_name": "Jen Gurecki", "guest_title": "CEO, Coalition Snow", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"There's not a team of marketing professionals sitting around a table trying to come up with a campaign, or what should we say. Everything you see and read and hear is 100% real. Real talk, from us, it's how we feel.\"", "podcast_length": "44:59"}, {"id": "57bedb77-8e40-4d90-a772-e5691e61e309", "episode_id": "a928ece0-f5c2-4649-8536-4dbd83497c09", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4848", "chunk_index": 13, "content": "Cole Heilborn (26:03): Yeah. One thing that I hope comes out of just the churn and some of the turmoil that we've seen in the industry is that hopefully people aren't afraid to make statements and aren't afraid to stand up and speak on their values. And I feel like a lot of brands sometimes have been hesitant to do that because they assume that it's easier just to play it, even play it cool. We're not going to say anything so that we don't piss off anyone. But I think people are starting to realize that that's maybe not how to succeed in the future. And like you said, that consumers want brands to be just like people and to have emotions and to say things for the companies out there that are really just looking internally and thinking, wow, do we need to really do it change up here? Do we need to shake up everything that we're thinking about our processes, what we're talking about, how we're talking about it? As someone who's, I don't know, I don't want to say has figured it out, but as someone who's just been in this space for a long time and isn't afraid to shake it up, what would you say to them?", "token_estimate": 279, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "26:03", "end_timestamp": "26:03", "podcast_index": 37, "episode_name": "Ep 37: Jen Gurecki | Snow for All | Coalition Sno‪w‬", "slug": "ep-37-jen-gurecki-snow-for-all-coalition-snow", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-37-jen-gurecki-snow-for-all-coalition-snow", "guest_name": "Jen Gurecki", "guest_title": "CEO, Coalition Snow", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"There's not a team of marketing professionals sitting around a table trying to come up with a campaign, or what should we say. Everything you see and read and hear is 100% real. Real talk, from us, it's how we feel.\"", "podcast_length": "44:59"}, {"id": "3c467570-cfb5-4593-ab39-ad3e4dfabcf0", "episode_id": "a928ece0-f5c2-4649-8536-4dbd83497c09", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4848", "chunk_index": 14, "content": "Jen Gurecki (27:21): I would say that we don't live in a time where you can be agnostic to the massive social, environmental, and economic problems that we face. Your consumers demand more of you. The world needs you, and stop being afraid and get over yourselves. You will make mistakes. You will say things that will make people angry. You will likely have to make public apologies. I have done many. They are embarrassing. And then you get over it and it makes you better because you learn from it and really stop focusing a hundred percent on the bottom line. If you cannot make that change. Now, the people who you're trying to appease now who don't want to hear these things, they're not going to be your customers in the next 10 years. We know by 2042, the majority of people in the United States will not identify as white. Start looking ahead if you're struggling, because you could make this as a values-based decision of there is an urgency around these issues and we want to contribute to positive change. Or you could just make a good business decision because you pay attention to the future and any way you slice it, you have to acknowledge that there's more than cisgendered straight white men participating in these activities. So you better figure out how to get all the other humans to enjoy your brand, and you likely will alienate people and okay, because at this stage of the game, you're going to alienate someone. So what side do you want to be on?\n\nCole Heilborn (29:06): I love it. If we're about out of time, we're reaching that 30 minute mark, are there any, I don't know, resources or leaders? You mentioned a few brands, but who are some people that you look up to that if people just kind of want to get exposed to more of this type of marketing, this type of philosophy and business, where could people go to learn more or just get a taste of it all?", "token_estimate": 471, "speakers": ["Jen Gurecki", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "27:21", "end_timestamp": "29:06", "podcast_index": 37, "episode_name": "Ep 37: Jen Gurecki | Snow for All | Coalition Sno‪w‬", "slug": "ep-37-jen-gurecki-snow-for-all-coalition-snow", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-37-jen-gurecki-snow-for-all-coalition-snow", "guest_name": "Jen Gurecki", "guest_title": "CEO, Coalition Snow", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"There's not a team of marketing professionals sitting around a table trying to come up with a campaign, or what should we say. Everything you see and read and hear is 100% real. Real talk, from us, it's how we feel.\"", "podcast_length": "44:59"}, {"id": "3a74fe7d-1b69-4837-a2ea-7b8aa9306909", "episode_id": "a928ece0-f5c2-4649-8536-4dbd83497c09", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4848", "chunk_index": 15, "content": "Jen Gurecki (29:33): So I'll give you a list of thought leaders, and they're not necessarily people that own brands. They don't have brands. They're not on that side of it, but they're thought leaders in the outdoor industry. And these are people that you can definitely learn from. So I would say Jose Gonzalez, Jenny Bruso of Unlikely Hikers, faith Briggs, let's see, who else? Rachel Ricketts, who's not in the industry, but is just incredible. I'm going to actually pull out my sisu magazine because there's so many people who we publish. If you look at Aaron Parisi from Transcending seven, Merrill is a brand I think is doing really well. Who else could I say? I would say Naomi Ren Berg from Ivory Toro. She's heavily involved in Van Life. There's so many. There's so many. Canoa Green, there's, there's a million. There's a million people right now who are saying really incredible things about enjoying being in the outdoors and loving the outdoors. Karen Ramos Ke How not to Travel like a basic bitch. Also all sorts of people. Is that helpful?\n\nCole Heilborn (31:19): That's super helpful. That's tons of places where people can get started. Thank you. Yeah. Alright. Well Jen, thanks again for taking the time to chat and share what you guys are up to. And I'm excited that you guys are out there trying to do things differently. God knows we could use some change. So thank you for everything that you and your team are doing. This was a pleasure\n\nJen Gurecki (31:41): Chatting. It's my pleasure. Thank you.\n\nCole Heilborn (31:44): Alright, well have a good rest of your day and enjoy the snow this season.\n\nJen Gurecki (31:49): I certainly will. Thank you.\n\nCole Heilborn (31:51): Alright, take care. Thanks again for listening to this episode. If you enjoyed it, please consider sharing it with a friend and leaving us a review on Apple. Also, remember to check out Darby Communications. If you're looking for a great PR and marketing team, go ahead and find 'em at darby communications.com.", "token_estimate": 502, "speakers": ["Jen Gurecki", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "29:33", "end_timestamp": "31:51", "podcast_index": 37, "episode_name": "Ep 37: Jen Gurecki | Snow for All | Coalition Sno‪w‬", "slug": "ep-37-jen-gurecki-snow-for-all-coalition-snow", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-37-jen-gurecki-snow-for-all-coalition-snow", "guest_name": "Jen Gurecki", "guest_title": "CEO, Coalition Snow", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"There's not a team of marketing professionals sitting around a table trying to come up with a campaign, or what should we say. Everything you see and read and hear is 100% real. Real talk, from us, it's how we feel.\"", "podcast_length": "44:59"}, {"id": "8bcb5b67-fa60-4ae6-9027-f5826c74f558", "episode_id": "8efa8786-3222-4d37-907c-69c3b145162a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484a", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:02): Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Helebo and I'll be your host today. This podcast dives deep with leaders of the outdoor marketing industry as they discuss the gritty details of their work, personal challenges and latest adventures, whether you're standing in the lift line, shuttling to fresh loam or cracking a local beer. I hope you enjoyed today's episode. This is the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. This episode is produced by Portside Productions, an outdoor, commercial and film production company based in the Pacific Northwest. Before we jump into today's episode, I wanted to talk about Darby Communications. They're our presenting sponsor for this episode. I've gotten to know them over the last few months as they've helped me with some PR and marketing for this podcast. The Darby Comm team has designed their process around my needs, combining a people first mindset with an analytical data-driven approach. (00:54): Today's business world moves fast, but the Darby Comm team has taken the time to truly get to know me and understand my goals. They've been a great partner to collaborate with and I'm really impressed with their well-rounded approach that generate real results that actually help bring this podcast to more years. If you're in the market for a great PR team in the outdoor wellness and fitness industry, go ahead and check 'em out@darbycommunications.com. Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Holborn. Today I'm sitting down with Rick Whitten break the CMO at Howler Bros. Rick, how are you?\n\nRick Wittenbraker (01:27): I'm great. Thanks for having me on. I appreciate the invite.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:30): Absolutely. Where are you calling in from today?\n\nRick Wittenbraker (01:34): We are based out of Austin, Texas. So that's where I'm calling you from actually today in our headquarters over here in the Clarksville neighborhood.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:44): Is this a first week back a few weeks back into the office?", "token_estimate": 506, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Rick Wittenbraker"], "start_timestamp": "00:02", "end_timestamp": "01:44", "podcast_index": 38, "episode_name": "Ep 38: Rick Wittenbraker | Community over Self | Howler Brother‪s‬", "slug": "ep-38-rick-wittenbraker-community-over-self-howler-brothers", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-38-rick-wittenbraker-community-over-self-howler-brothers", "guest_name": "Rick Wittenbraker", "guest_title": "CMO,Howler Brothers", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Being nimble, not thinking you have the answers, being willing to adapt, these are all things that at any moment under duress, I think are opportunities for someone to not just survive, but thrive.\"", "podcast_length": "44:60"}, {"id": "2235077b-dbff-4563-8236-9984f9d24b62", "episode_id": "8efa8786-3222-4d37-907c-69c3b145162a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484a", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Rick Wittenbraker (01:48): No, I've actually been coming back since maybe late July, early August. Not everyone is just sort of a select few. And during quarantine, we primarily left our office to the design team just because they have so many physical samples and swatches and fabrics and stuff that they had all this need for the physical stuff, and so we sort of left it to them, but we have enough space and we're kind of spread out enough that I can be in and out and not interfere with them or in any sort of risk there. So I've been coming back. I'm grateful for resemblance of normalcy in a daily routine, and it's been good to, at least across the room, have some interactions with folks. That's been good and helpful.\n\nCole Heilborn (02:49): Yeah, that's got to lift a lot of morale too.\n\nRick Wittenbraker (02:53): Yeah, it's good. And I think everybody's probably having these same challenges. You can certainly do a ton via Zoom and calls and texts and Skype and email, but I think what you miss are the interactions, and what we've found is that we're able to do everything, but it's kind of twice the squeeze for the same amount of juice. And when we're all together, it's like we maybe we got a question about how we want to do this or what's our approach on X, and if we're all together, we can knock it out 15 minutes. If it's all over Slack, well then that's like, oh my gosh, like a day and a half to get everything finalized. So for the interactions we have been having, I'm very grateful for it. It makes me feel like we're getting more done each day and it's less just the tedium of always being on Slack.\n\nCole Heilborn (03:59): Yeah, yeah. It's good when you can feel like you're actually getting somewhere when you're cranking away and the miles actually start to run behind you. That's a good feeling. So I'm really curious to learn a little bit more about the brand and who you are, who the team is, doing a quick search and going to your website and looking at the Howler Bros crew. You guys, I love your direction, your vibe. You've got a very serious but fun tone. So I'd love to kind of dive into a little bit of who the brand is.", "token_estimate": 541, "speakers": ["Rick Wittenbraker", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "01:48", "end_timestamp": "03:59", "podcast_index": 38, "episode_name": "Ep 38: Rick Wittenbraker | Community over Self | Howler Brother‪s‬", "slug": "ep-38-rick-wittenbraker-community-over-self-howler-brothers", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-38-rick-wittenbraker-community-over-self-howler-brothers", "guest_name": "Rick Wittenbraker", "guest_title": "CMO,Howler Brothers", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Being nimble, not thinking you have the answers, being willing to adapt, these are all things that at any moment under duress, I think are opportunities for someone to not just survive, but thrive.\"", "podcast_length": "44:60"}, {"id": "d18f89e5-96a5-4241-89b2-b7217a1de132", "episode_id": "8efa8786-3222-4d37-907c-69c3b145162a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484a", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Rick Wittenbraker (04:34): Yeah, well, Haller Brothers was started by Chase Herd and Andy Stepanian, two of my partners and really good friends and probably two of the most creatively talented people I've ever met. And so they went to UVA together, they are still in a band together and being in the band and having the creative collaboration of music and then the work of making the band successful and touring and all this interaction together and stuff. So they have the sort of creative collaboration together. And then they had the sort of effort work side of that together. And it was, without going into the full encyclopedic history, there was an opportunity. Chase is one of the most talented people in the world and he was a fine art painter. He then decided he wanted to go an architect, so he went and got his master's in architecture and was a practicing architect. (06:01): And this is all simultaneous to the band, but so Chase and through his painting and his drawings and stuff had been asked by another apparel brand a long, long time ago to come up with some designs and he did. And then they kind of went off into the sunset and never ended up making his designs. And he was really bummed because he really was fired up to have these things come to life and to wear these shirts and things. And so he just never got the chance. And so that sort of sparked this idea of, well, what if we went and made those? Well, if we're going to make those, maybe we should, this certainly the band project, this is a separate initiative, and if we're going to do that, then maybe we should start our little company. Well, if we're going to do that, then we should really put some thought and effort behind what is this brand and what does it stand for and what do we want it to be? (07:11): And so he and Andy, I give them all the credit in the world. They really spent a lot of time fine tuning and crafting that even way before they were launched publicly. And so there was a lot of back and forth and a lot of collaboration there and a lot of hemming and hawing on what it is and what it's not. And one of the things that the timing of wanting to do this combined with these other points of inspiration for them and things that they love to do, and where it all kind of ended up was they felt like there was an opportunity, an opening some white space to create apparel that sort of paid homage to a lot of the vintage stuff that they loved wearing, but also had the performance capabilities of a lot of the more technical stuff out there. (08:25): And so that you, in the essence was that you could go do any of these activities you wanted to do. In this instance, a lot of it was around surfing, saltwater fly fishing, being active up in the mountains on a given day might involve five activities and doing all those things but still wearing stuff that you felt was cool and look good. And now we sort of jokingly say has to work just as well in the bar as it does on the water. But I think especially back then it was, I don't want to say, oh, I'm going to do this activity, which means all this specialized clothing that I then as soon as I'm done doing that, I feel like a total dork in and I want to go change, and then we go do something else, I got to change again. And so it was really like, Hey, what can I wear all day? (09:20): It looks great, it's comfortable, is very designed forward, but in a laid back way, a lot of attention to detail, a lot of bringing design and creativity forward instead of pushing it to the back. And so that's kind of where they landed. And then the other thing really, you had asked about the brand and the brand part of it was they wanted to do something that wasn't about, it wasn't ego driven, it wasn't about me. Look what I accomplished, look what I did. It wasn't man versus nature at all. In fact, it was sort of the opposite, like, hey, if we go to this place, we can take this trip to go surfing and it's flat. We're still going to have an awesome trip even though the waves were flat or fishing, we're going to go take this trip with our buddies, but even if we don't catch a damn thing, it's still going to be an awesome trip because we're on this adventure with our buddies and it's really about the shared experience. (10:39): That's why it's brothers plural and not singular or not like me or a single noun there. And really about just these, because the moments in between tend to be a lot of the memories that you make and the best times and the stuff you laugh about. And when you think back on a trip from 5, 8, 10 years ago, it's less about a single instance and more about the sort of collection, this sort of photo album, if you will, of all these memories. And the stuff that probably stands out the most is either the funny stuff or the inside jokes that came out of that trip or the flat tire in the middle of nowhere or whatever. And so those things, and really not only appreciating those things, but almost like doubling down on those things is sort of the ethos that came out of a lot of this and that it's shared. It's about the adventure along the way and the journey and the moments in between is really where all that came from.", "token_estimate": 1303, "speakers": ["Rick Wittenbraker"], "start_timestamp": "04:34", "end_timestamp": "04:34", "podcast_index": 38, "episode_name": "Ep 38: Rick Wittenbraker | Community over Self | Howler Brother‪s‬", "slug": "ep-38-rick-wittenbraker-community-over-self-howler-brothers", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-38-rick-wittenbraker-community-over-self-howler-brothers", "guest_name": "Rick Wittenbraker", "guest_title": "CMO,Howler Brothers", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Being nimble, not thinking you have the answers, being willing to adapt, these are all things that at any moment under duress, I think are opportunities for someone to not just survive, but thrive.\"", "podcast_length": "44:60"}, {"id": "ab7d286d-c800-414f-a2c9-643372458753", "episode_id": "8efa8786-3222-4d37-907c-69c3b145162a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484a", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Cole Heilborn (11:57): Yeah, interesting. How does that mentality, the idea that it's about the experiences, about the memories, how does that affect, I dunno, the brand's decisions or how does that affect the decisions you make in the day-to-day today?\n\nRick Wittenbraker (12:13): Well, and since the launching and we've gone on to further refine and define this, and I think what's equally important is to define what it's not and what we, what do not do. And I think that really matters as far as putting clarity around it, especially for the team. We don't necessarily broadcast all the things that we're on the not list, but we do that from a practice standpoint and it gives you a good sort of check to say, Hey, okay, this image or this design or this post or whatever in the world it's going to be, is this checking all the yes list and then does this have any infraction on the no list? Because that's a good check. It's also a great way to help. Our brand is very nuanced and a lot of people can get 80 or 90% of the way there, but that last 10% is the real detail. We put a lot of effort into this so that when we bring new people onto the team or we're working with a third party, it really helps to articulate that better and we can get them up to speed faster.\n\nCole Heilborn (13:45): Yeah. Okay. So what would you say the average person can get 80% there? What's that 20 or 10% that they miss? And then how do you guide them with your knots and the yeses and the nos?", "token_estimate": 371, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Rick Wittenbraker"], "start_timestamp": "11:57", "end_timestamp": "13:45", "podcast_index": 38, "episode_name": "Ep 38: Rick Wittenbraker | Community over Self | Howler Brother‪s‬", "slug": "ep-38-rick-wittenbraker-community-over-self-howler-brothers", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-38-rick-wittenbraker-community-over-self-howler-brothers", "guest_name": "Rick Wittenbraker", "guest_title": "CMO,Howler Brothers", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Being nimble, not thinking you have the answers, being willing to adapt, these are all things that at any moment under duress, I think are opportunities for someone to not just survive, but thrive.\"", "podcast_length": "44:60"}, {"id": "3a06f02d-3dab-46c5-b825-8dff50343c98", "episode_id": "8efa8786-3222-4d37-907c-69c3b145162a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484a", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Rick Wittenbraker (14:02): Yeah, well, I mean I think that there's kind of two sides to that coin. One is what are they interpreting about us if they were to go try and type it up? And with regard to that, I think one of the best things we can do is in fact be anything we're putting out into the world, whether that's a product or a message or an ad or whatever it is that all fits within our framework. And so if you really went to study all this stuff, you would start to see these commonalities or these things and you would say, oh yeah, that feels very howery. And then simultaneously this thing over here, or it's close, but it doesn't, just as a simple example, we a long time ago made the decision that we weren't going to post, we're not about grip and grins, and it's not that we don't like to see somebody holding up this fish. It really was just about, it's not about me, which at the end of the day, the grip and grin is kind of about me. (15:28): So it's not about me and it's about this shared experience rather. I think we would probably rather post something that was, I don't know, the moment before that or the moment after that or it got away or we broke the rod or whatever it is, the high five afterwards or something like that. Having a beer after you let the fish go, I mean something like that is probably more in our sweet spot. But the other side of that is just that almost every other brand that interacts with phishing posts or utilizes the grip and grid, it doesn't just have to be a social post. And so we wanted it to always be like, man, that's a, if you covered up the logo on the ad or you didn't know who shared that or whatever, that you still should be about 95% positive that that's us and not someone else. (16:43): So a part of this is the perception, and so where we try to control or manage that is through what we're putting out. And again, that's why we wanted to define all this stuff. The other side of that coin though is I do believe that there is, and that's sort of people looking at us was the first side and the second side is people acting on their own. And the fact that someone who likes Hower Brothers wants to incorporate us into their world, they might do stuff that we're ambivalent about or I don't know, well, we don't really do some activity that they love to do, but but they want to incorporate us in that. That's awesome. We love that. And to that regard, if they want to get real fired up about wearing their howlers gear in their grip and grin photo, hell yes, that's awesome. That's them. And so we fully embrace that and it's not a we're against this type of thing, it's more just like, Hey, we made a creative decision. We didn't want to do this. The fact that our audience wants to do that, like hell yeah. That's awesome. And that's them being usually is pretty happy and we're a part of that happy moment then hell yes. That's great.", "token_estimate": 728, "speakers": ["Rick Wittenbraker"], "start_timestamp": "14:02", "end_timestamp": "14:02", "podcast_index": 38, "episode_name": "Ep 38: Rick Wittenbraker | Community over Self | Howler Brother‪s‬", "slug": "ep-38-rick-wittenbraker-community-over-self-howler-brothers", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-38-rick-wittenbraker-community-over-self-howler-brothers", "guest_name": "Rick Wittenbraker", "guest_title": "CMO,Howler Brothers", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Being nimble, not thinking you have the answers, being willing to adapt, these are all things that at any moment under duress, I think are opportunities for someone to not just survive, but thrive.\"", "podcast_length": "44:60"}, {"id": "6576f7cd-35da-4841-9815-e0d88ca5fa97", "episode_id": "8efa8786-3222-4d37-907c-69c3b145162a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484a", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Cole Heilborn (18:20): Yeah. So when you're having these conversations with the internal team, I want to lay some groundwork or some framework as we move forwards in this episode. As you're having these conversations with the team and you're talking about what the brand is, what the brand isn't, how do you first define not necessarily your brand, but a brand as you're having these conversations?\n\nRick Wittenbraker (18:43): Oh man. I think first off I'll say I think the word brand is totally over and misused this day and age. And I think it's people misuse it to mean the company. People misuse it to mean logo. I think there's a lot of ways that it's sort of misused. I like to think of the brand as at a very high level as sort of the personality. And if you think about it like a character in a movie or a book or a story that it is a character and that character has attributes and does things a certain way, and you have a really good actor that can really get into a character. And it's not them, it's the character. And I think that is really what this is. And so we can go right or define a character and it has a way they talk, it has a way they act, it has a way they move, it has emotions, it has all these things, and that makes up this character. (20:15): It's not just it one of those, it's not just the way it looks. It's not just the way it talks. It's not just a logo or colors, it's not logo usage guidelines. And I think where it gets hard is you have, there's something like a consumer facing business like ours and something that makes a tactile physical good like ours, you have way more interaction with a person or your audience than say a software Dropbox, for example, which is software that I like. But other than that little blue box logo, there's not a whole lot that is from the brand, you know what I mean? And so it's pretty sterile and it's about trying to be easy to use and functional and all that, but just picking colors and using a logo isn't really cutting the mustard, at least in our world. And so we think the better, the more thought out we can make that and the more deeper and richer, and it doesn't even have to be articulated from a definition standpoint, it just has to be, I think really again, back to that character example, it has to be really well-written, so to speak. (21:59): And look, we always want to make it better than what we're doing and we're always striving for more, but hopefully there's a tone that comes out in our copy and in the products we make and in the videos we do and in everything that we put out and the products, and that's different than the next brand.", "token_estimate": 660, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Rick Wittenbraker"], "start_timestamp": "18:20", "end_timestamp": "18:43", "podcast_index": 38, "episode_name": "Ep 38: Rick Wittenbraker | Community over Self | Howler Brother‪s‬", "slug": "ep-38-rick-wittenbraker-community-over-self-howler-brothers", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-38-rick-wittenbraker-community-over-self-howler-brothers", "guest_name": "Rick Wittenbraker", "guest_title": "CMO,Howler Brothers", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Being nimble, not thinking you have the answers, being willing to adapt, these are all things that at any moment under duress, I think are opportunities for someone to not just survive, but thrive.\"", "podcast_length": "44:60"}, {"id": "58e22c15-a2e5-4be2-b5af-3dae673b6e6d", "episode_id": "8efa8786-3222-4d37-907c-69c3b145162a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484a", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Cole Heilborn (22:25): Yeah, yeah, that's a good point. Maybe it isn't so much about defining the brand, but it's more about being able to say, does this fit the brand and say yes or no? Like you mentioned someone's personality, like you think about a friend you have who maybe has a personality that stands out and you can say, oh, does this clothing, does this activity, does this fit this person? And the things that they might enjoy, and you might not be able to articulate why or how it fits them, but you can say, yes, this works, or no, this doesn't.\n\nRick Wittenbraker (23:03): Yeah. I think again, it goes to that interpretation from audience and team is a part of that audience. They're just more intimately involved and sometimes on the inner circle inside of that character. But it's just like to use this sort of nerdy example, it's not Harrison forward, we're talking about Han Solo. And so every time that you see Han Solo, it needs to be with that smart ass sort of commentary with it's a little cavalier, it's a little this he operates, we know he is a little bit of a troublemaker, but he's got a good heart. It's all those things. That's all part of his character. And if if a scene came up and it was all of a sudden just Harrison Ford and that's not going to cut it. (24:23): So we really try to impart that on the team and we have to, if you really think that through it is in the products, it is in all the content and things we're putting out, it comes to the way we handle customer service and interact with people. It comes to the way we interact with people in our stores and it is a visual thing. And there's just like any person they have the way they act, the way they speak, the way they dress, the music, they listen to the stuff they like. And so all that, we try to sort of paint a full picture with that.", "token_estimate": 460, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Rick Wittenbraker"], "start_timestamp": "22:25", "end_timestamp": "23:03", "podcast_index": 38, "episode_name": "Ep 38: Rick Wittenbraker | Community over Self | Howler Brother‪s‬", "slug": "ep-38-rick-wittenbraker-community-over-self-howler-brothers", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-38-rick-wittenbraker-community-over-self-howler-brothers", "guest_name": "Rick Wittenbraker", "guest_title": "CMO,Howler Brothers", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Being nimble, not thinking you have the answers, being willing to adapt, these are all things that at any moment under duress, I think are opportunities for someone to not just survive, but thrive.\"", "podcast_length": "44:60"}, {"id": "bb7771db-14e9-41e2-8157-c9b7695e6588", "episode_id": "8efa8786-3222-4d37-907c-69c3b145162a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484a", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Cole Heilborn (25:11): We've kind of laid the groundwork I think for how you would describe a brand, what it is, what it isn't, maybe ways to think about it back in March when the world rapidly evolving around everyone, you guys, everyone in the outdoor industry. How did brand, did you guys think about what was happening and what you should do or how you should act through the lens of the brand?\n\nRick Wittenbraker (25:33): Yeah. Well, that's a really great question and I think there's kind of two parts to that. One is what do we do as a company? And we as a company did certain things and maybe added things or changed things or we had to revisit just our financial situation and how are we going to operate and what are we going to do? What changes need to be made? We had to do that just like anybody else. It doesn't matter what your company is or what your business is. And so we did a lot of that. We were very, I think very fortunate that we're in a good position and didn't have to make any sort of drastic cuts and didn't have to do anything that had a long-term detriment to the business. So we're very glad about that. We did also from a brand perspective though, and from the Voice, we did decide that we wanted to come out and message everybody. And now that seems like 10 years ago that all that happened. But looking back on it, we really just wanted to tell everybody, Hey, we're all in this together. We're with you. We're still going to be out there. We're not going to all of a sudden turn into something else, but we hear you and I don't know necessarily what we can do, but we're here to help. (27:25): And so I think it was sort of a welcome when we sent that first email out and we weren't trying to sell anything and we weren't trying to get you to come watch some new video, we were just saying, Hey, we know you're going through this. We're all going through this, we're all in this together. We got a lot of feedback that it was a very welcome and appreciated message, even though we weren't really trying to accomplish anything. I think what it maybe reaffirmed in people's heads is like, yeah, these are good folks and they're not just in it to try and shove me to the cash register and they're not going to change who they are just because they're under duress and that we're going to march on. And so we did end up after that. We ended up doing a couple different efforts that helped some groups in need, especially around here in Texas. (28:36): And that was great, and it was a real easy decision. We actually had this, it was our annual collaboration with Helm's workshop. We had all the product ready, we had all the assets ready, and we were ready to push go on this thing. And it's usually one of those things that sells out in no time. And we said, Hey, we don't feel right and it doesn't feel authentic for us to just act like all this stuff isn't happening and we're just going to kick this launch out the door and say, yeah, yeah, now we're back to normal. Give us your money. And so we said, look, I think this is a no brainer. This needs to benefit some groups in need. And that was still so early on in the whole Corona scenario that we didn't have yet a full understanding of the scope and the breadth, but we made it around these two Texas groups that were organized and help. (29:44): And so it was great, and it felt right, it felt good. And then from there we proceeded to get involved in a lot of other fundraising efforts and contributed to a lot of other campaigns and then even sent a bunch of old inventory to be turned into masks. We didn't make masks from scratch, but we said, Hey, we have these friends that this is what they do. They cut and sew stuff and then they're sort of converting their efforts into making masks, so why don't we just give you all this material? And so we were able to work with, actually there's three that we did that with our friends, Fort Lonesome here in Austin. They're sort of our longtime friends and collaborators, a really awesome lady named Anna Ergot out of California who makes surfboard bags and she's used to making 'em out of other fabric and those awesome coffee bean bags, those jute bags, so it's called Sagebrush board bags. (31:11): And so she makes those and used to repurposing materials. So I reached out, we had sort of connected in the past and I reached out to her and said, here's what we'd like to do. And so she made these incredible masks and it was really neat and we didn't ask, we don't want them, we want you to sell 'em as a individual maker. You can sell 'em Fort Lonesome, same thing. Some of theirs. They ended up, I think donating all the profits from the sale of the mass to organizations that needed help. And the third was a friend's wife who is a really dynamic sower, but she's also a healthcare professional. And so she actually supplied masks to a lot of her coworkers in the Houston area. So for us it was like, Hey, here's a cool way to get involved. It's not about us. I mean, we were sharing their efforts, but we weren't making any money from it. And so I think that was just more kind of our perspective on, hey, we still need to be us. We're all going through this thing. It sucks. We still need to be us. We can't all of a sudden become something we are not and we still want to march on and help others that are marching on as well.", "token_estimate": 1344, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Rick Wittenbraker"], "start_timestamp": "25:11", "end_timestamp": "25:33", "podcast_index": 38, "episode_name": "Ep 38: Rick Wittenbraker | Community over Self | Howler Brother‪s‬", "slug": "ep-38-rick-wittenbraker-community-over-self-howler-brothers", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-38-rick-wittenbraker-community-over-self-howler-brothers", "guest_name": "Rick Wittenbraker", "guest_title": "CMO,Howler Brothers", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Being nimble, not thinking you have the answers, being willing to adapt, these are all things that at any moment under duress, I think are opportunities for someone to not just survive, but thrive.\"", "podcast_length": "44:60"}, {"id": "b11ba9d8-1737-41c6-aa64-52b0027d424b", "episode_id": "8efa8786-3222-4d37-907c-69c3b145162a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484a", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Cole Heilborn (32:36): Was that a tough decision? You said the company internally was looking at expenses and budgets and trying to figure out how is this going to impact us financially? Can we keep the team around? But then you're also saying, well, let's just give away a bunch of product and inventory and help out where we can. Was that a tough decision? You're looking at giving away stuff, but you're also looking, well, can we keep the people on the team at the same time?\n\nRick Wittenbraker (33:07): Yeah, it really wasn't. I think we knew that this inventory at the end of the day wasn't going to make or break us. And so hey, that's a pretty easy decision. (33:22): And simultaneously we're helping these other groups who have been our friends prior to this, so we're just helping our community. And at the end of the day, that's why it's brothers plural is it's about community and shared experiences. And so it's like, Hey, this is a perfect example of that. It's just not as cool or as fun as the trips we like to take. But it's nonetheless, it's about being with and helping others. And then I think from the business perspective, we had the same reactions as everybody else initially, everybody's overcome by fear and back then everyone was scrambling for toilet paper and all that stuff. I think though, once we just like everybody else, we had similar thoughts. And then I think once we kind of calmed down and caught our breath, we said, Hey, here's our situation. We're going to make some changes. But we were able to do that. That didn't affect our full-time\n\nSpeaker 3 (34:38): Personnel.", "token_estimate": 403, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Rick Wittenbraker", "Speaker 3"], "start_timestamp": "32:36", "end_timestamp": "34:38", "podcast_index": 38, "episode_name": "Ep 38: Rick Wittenbraker | Community over Self | Howler Brother‪s‬", "slug": "ep-38-rick-wittenbraker-community-over-self-howler-brothers", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-38-rick-wittenbraker-community-over-self-howler-brothers", "guest_name": "Rick Wittenbraker", "guest_title": "CMO,Howler Brothers", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Being nimble, not thinking you have the answers, being willing to adapt, these are all things that at any moment under duress, I think are opportunities for someone to not just survive, but thrive.\"", "podcast_length": "44:60"}, {"id": "a6532506-e47e-4346-8c82-cd7da57d5e03", "episode_id": "8efa8786-3222-4d37-907c-69c3b145162a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484a", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Rick Wittenbraker (34:39): It did affect our shop personnel, but the shops were closed, (34:45): But that's sort of natural. But we made some business decisions around it and it changed maybe what we would have done during this time and maybe how much or what we made back then, which is now affecting us here and six eight months later. But all those things we made after we had sort of calmed down and everyone sort of caught their breath and normalized a little bit. And then we said, I think that despite all this, us hiding in a cave is not going to help us or them, anybody else. And so I think we need to get out there, but we need to be very mindful of the scenario and situation people are going through. (35:39): And so we also don't just want to go guns ablazing and act like nothing has happened. And so I think being mindful sometimes that's how you communicate what you communicate and then what we did. And so I think once we started putting stuff out there and we got back to our regular routine, there was an incredible reception. I think one of those things was that people were, I'm trying to think of a great way to frame this, but I think people were stuck at home and maybe a little stressed and maybe a little working from home and figuring out all this stuff and it's strain and stress and there was a little bit of escape. Oh man, I threw that funky shirt from Hower and I think I'm just going to order that or whatever. And I think there was also a bit of optimism that man, when this storm passes, I am going to go take that trip or I'm going to go do some adventure. And so there was this man, I want to get this because I want to be geared up. And even if it's not something you don't buy anything new, maybe it just said, you know what, man, I am going to go do that cross country trip or whatever it is. It doesn't matter. And so I think we got a very welcome response and reception once we did get back out there.", "token_estimate": 487, "speakers": ["Rick Wittenbraker"], "start_timestamp": "34:39", "end_timestamp": "34:39", "podcast_index": 38, "episode_name": "Ep 38: Rick Wittenbraker | Community over Self | Howler Brother‪s‬", "slug": "ep-38-rick-wittenbraker-community-over-self-howler-brothers", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-38-rick-wittenbraker-community-over-self-howler-brothers", "guest_name": "Rick Wittenbraker", "guest_title": "CMO,Howler Brothers", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Being nimble, not thinking you have the answers, being willing to adapt, these are all things that at any moment under duress, I think are opportunities for someone to not just survive, but thrive.\"", "podcast_length": "44:60"}, {"id": "52f41259-c8b9-4c42-aca3-8314734d645f", "episode_id": "8efa8786-3222-4d37-907c-69c3b145162a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484a", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Cole Heilborn (37:17): What have you seen now that we're in October and we're what, eight months into this, obviously the industry, a lot of the industry seem to have gotten really busy really fast and now there's manufacturing issues. What have you observed?\n\nRick Wittenbraker (37:41): I think we've seen a whole host of different things, and a lot of that has been, there's slowed load, there's been a lot of, from manufacturing and logistics standpoints, there's been a lot of challenges and a lot of delays and things that you used to take 10 days now take 15 days or whatever it is. And so that's had to account for a lot of that. And it means that we've had to be nimble and flexible on a lot of different fronts, not just our back of house, but also the front of house and say, okay, well man, just because we had originally planned to launch this new stuff on the first, we might have to shuffle some things around and now push that to the 20th and do something else today. So I think it's just being able to adapt and adjust and not being so rigid because everything has changed. I mean the good news is that everybody's in the same boat, so everyone has an understanding of why things are maybe a little different than they normally were or than what they had expected. But I think that you can also use that to your benefit in a very sort of open and healthy way and say, okay, well hey man, it's all different now. So we're going to do things a little differently today. (39:15): And I think there was one example was we try to set a high bar for ourselves on the creative side and on the content side. And we took a look at this and said, well, we still want to be creative. We still want to be putting things out, but maybe that needs to look a little different. Maybe it's like, Hey, we're trying to do these, we're also all quarantining, we're individually personally, but also working from home just like everybody else. And so we said, okay, and reached out to the ambassadors and friends and said, what are you doing during quarantine? And so what we ended up coming up with was this little series of videos and we dubbed it the Howler Hut and it was all shot on iPhones. And I don't think anyone expected it to be win an Academy Award, but it was like, Hey, this is still fun, still interesting, still pretty cool. And we can still add quality on top of that and get it to a level where we think is good and interesting. And it is just an example of you got to be nimble and you got to iterate and think a little outside the box. And", "token_estimate": 639, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Rick Wittenbraker"], "start_timestamp": "37:17", "end_timestamp": "37:41", "podcast_index": 38, "episode_name": "Ep 38: Rick Wittenbraker | Community over Self | Howler Brother‪s‬", "slug": "ep-38-rick-wittenbraker-community-over-self-howler-brothers", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-38-rick-wittenbraker-community-over-self-howler-brothers", "guest_name": "Rick Wittenbraker", "guest_title": "CMO,Howler Brothers", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Being nimble, not thinking you have the answers, being willing to adapt, these are all things that at any moment under duress, I think are opportunities for someone to not just survive, but thrive.\"", "podcast_length": "44:60"}, {"id": "540c203d-0219-4d2e-b19e-3bae0d40a532", "episode_id": "8efa8786-3222-4d37-907c-69c3b145162a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484a", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Cole Heilborn (40:44): It almost sounds like the way you guys reacted and are reacting is kind of true to who you are. You described the brand and what it is. It's not about the victory or bagging the fish, but it's about the mess ups and running out of gas along the way and figuring out how to make it home. And it kind of sounds like that's how you're reacting in this too. Well, it is different, so we're just going to roll with it.\n\nRick Wittenbraker (41:10): Yeah, that's funny. I do think that there was definitely some of that and there was definitely a little bit of what are you going to do? We could sit here and pout about it. We could sit here and act like, and we all got, I think everyone received, we all get emails from companies and brands that we buy from or support or whatever. And I think if you go back and did a little study on that, I think there's brands that tried to act like nothing was happening because they just wanted their business to continue and they wanted revenue to continue. And they maybe out of fear, but we're just send it anyways. We're going to send that email to anyways, we're just going to march ahead and just stay the course. And that's one option. (42:05): I think we tried to be a little humble with it and a little like, Hey, this sucks. We're all in this together. That was a little more of our stance, but I think we internally too, and there was some trial and error about what works working remotely. We had never really done that as a team. And so we had to figure some stuff out, how to figure out how to do photo shoots in a quarantine environment. And so I think we've learned a lot along the way. We've figured out some things that work, we've figured out some things that don't work, found some ways to maybe do stuff a little scrappier and more effective down the road even if things do get back to normal.", "token_estimate": 468, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Rick Wittenbraker"], "start_timestamp": "40:44", "end_timestamp": "41:10", "podcast_index": 38, "episode_name": "Ep 38: Rick Wittenbraker | Community over Self | Howler Brother‪s‬", "slug": "ep-38-rick-wittenbraker-community-over-self-howler-brothers", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-38-rick-wittenbraker-community-over-self-howler-brothers", "guest_name": "Rick Wittenbraker", "guest_title": "CMO,Howler Brothers", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Being nimble, not thinking you have the answers, being willing to adapt, these are all things that at any moment under duress, I think are opportunities for someone to not just survive, but thrive.\"", "podcast_length": "44:60"}, {"id": "dce72d43-f52d-4c15-840e-a33ef1420ff5", "episode_id": "8efa8786-3222-4d37-907c-69c3b145162a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484a", "chunk_index": 12, "content": "Cole Heilborn (42:58): Yeah. One of the things that I think I've enjoyed and that out of the bad, there's been I think a lot of good things where you see examples of good people doing good things. When you think about this brand, this personality that you and your team are kind of helping steer, what type of responsibility does it have in general, but also in today's climate, in today's pandemic age or year where leadership and role models and examples are maybe more important now than they were last year?", "token_estimate": 128, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "42:58", "end_timestamp": "42:58", "podcast_index": 38, "episode_name": "Ep 38: Rick Wittenbraker | Community over Self | Howler Brother‪s‬", "slug": "ep-38-rick-wittenbraker-community-over-self-howler-brothers", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-38-rick-wittenbraker-community-over-self-howler-brothers", "guest_name": "Rick Wittenbraker", "guest_title": "CMO,Howler Brothers", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Being nimble, not thinking you have the answers, being willing to adapt, these are all things that at any moment under duress, I think are opportunities for someone to not just survive, but thrive.\"", "podcast_length": "44:60"}, {"id": "61d6e633-8531-4527-856d-f8b50c509a20", "episode_id": "8efa8786-3222-4d37-907c-69c3b145162a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484a", "chunk_index": 13, "content": "Rick Wittenbraker (43:46): Yeah, that's a great question. I think there's definitely more things you have to be mindful of, even the idea of like, oh, can we have two people in a photograph together less than six feet apart? And if you were being super CY, a and restrictive, you could say no, but where we got to was like, well, yeah, I mean, what if these two guys are roommates and they've lived together the whole time, or they're best friends and they said they're going to quarantine together, I don't know, whatever they're brothers. And so you can't necessarily define it just by looking at a picture or you don't necessarily have all the information. I think the other side of that is that we always wanted to, even with all these restrictions, we still wanted to have this high bar for ourselves from a creative standpoint. And so if we still want to have that high bar yet, it's a lot more difficult to achieve that, then it's going to take some effort and some trial and error to figure out how to accomplish that. (45:16): And so we've had to do some things about, alright, well let's go reimagine what this video shoot looks like. Let's go reimagine what a photo shoot looks like or how we're designing products or how we're doing anything. So I think we've just had to, from our own perspective, take a look at those things. I think from a responsibility standpoint, you definitely want to just one more pass through before you put it out there. Are we overlooking, are we being just because, oh yeah, that's a great photo, we should put that out there. But are we overlooking the fact that, oh, it looks like are we being insensitive to Corona or COVID or whatever you want to call it, quarantine. I think on the same side, there's a lot of people that said, or a lot of businesses that said, we're going to start making branded masks or we're going to start selling these masks. And I don't think that's a bad thing. We just decided we didn't want to do it. And then it wasn't something that we were in the sense of, oh, we're shifting gears. We're only going to make, we are only going to make masks for a branding standpoint. (46:48): That wasn't us. So I think we just said we're going to, Hey, we'll contribute to these efforts that our friends are doing. We will contribute to, we'll go get some buffs made and put our designs on 'em. But we probably would've done that anyways from a sunshield standpoint. So I don't know. I mean, look, I don't think there's a right answer. I think it definitely put in our minds like, Hey, we might need to think about some of these things. There's an extra layer, a few extra check boxes we got to go through before we push something out", "token_estimate": 674, "speakers": ["Rick Wittenbraker"], "start_timestamp": "43:46", "end_timestamp": "43:46", "podcast_index": 38, "episode_name": "Ep 38: Rick Wittenbraker | Community over Self | Howler Brother‪s‬", "slug": "ep-38-rick-wittenbraker-community-over-self-howler-brothers", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-38-rick-wittenbraker-community-over-self-howler-brothers", "guest_name": "Rick Wittenbraker", "guest_title": "CMO,Howler Brothers", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Being nimble, not thinking you have the answers, being willing to adapt, these are all things that at any moment under duress, I think are opportunities for someone to not just survive, but thrive.\"", "podcast_length": "44:60"}, {"id": "23a01f32-2bac-4d1f-82d9-58bedb1007a3", "episode_id": "8efa8786-3222-4d37-907c-69c3b145162a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484a", "chunk_index": 14, "content": "Cole Heilborn (47:30): There. Yeah. Alright, so one of my last questions we're coming up on the end of our time for today, and I'll ask the question, I don't know that the question is as simple. The answer is as simple as the question is, why will strong brands come out of this pandemic, whatever you want to call it this last year, why will they come out strong versus maybe a company who doesn't have the same, I dunno, philosophy or approach to how they do business?\n\nRick Wittenbraker (48:04): Yeah, I think there's kind of two sides to that. And you said brands, I think I'll answer on both the perspective of companies and brands. (48:18): I think from a company perspective, there's certainly things like your financial position or what is your burn rate or what are all these sort of company business items or metrics that mattered even prior to all this and that this has stress tested a lot of those things. And so I think what you're going to see some businesses that were maybe not in a very healthy position or in a very, they had extremely thin margins and things like that and they may not make it. And I think if anything, this high stress or strange set of circumstances has just accelerated that timeline. They may have been headed towards closing shop or headed towards some point where they were going to need to sell the business or do something else. And I think this has probably just accelerated that. And I think that the other side is that there's businesses that were in a healthy spot and have been able to say, well man, we are in a pretty healthy spot. We fortunate, so let's rethink this and let's be, how do we stay in a healthy spot? (49:49): What are the things we need to do to make sure we don't go away? And so I think from a business perspective, there's a lot there. And again, I go back to being nimble, not thinking you have all the answers, being willing to adapt. And those are all things that under any moment of duress I think are opportunities for someone to not just survive but thrive. And then I would say from the brand perspective, kind of what I mentioned earlier, but I think there's probably a lot of brands that panicked and either put their head in the sand or tried to act like nothing was happening and we got to march along or all of a sudden started acting very differently. And so if someone you knew started acting very differently, you would say, man, what's up with Jimmy? (50:56): He's acting weird. He's all of a sudden real irrational or erratic or always asking me if he can borrow money. You're like, man, what the hell is wrong with Jimmy? And so I think there's going to be some sort of, because with any relationship and that a brand of the customer or audience member is, there's a relationship there. And so either it may feel inauthentic or it may feel like a strained or pressured or schizophrenic or whatever. So I think you're going to see some of that or we have seen some of that. And so what the fallout of that is if they've lost some trust or they've lost some effectiveness, that's something that we may or have or will see. I also think that there's in the best way and the best opportunity, it's an opportunity for all these brands to be real humble and honest and open and be like, man, we're over here slugging it out too. So we feel you and here's what we're doing. And I think even just that open this or maybe a little transparency or maybe they're sharing more than they would have a little peek behind the curtain a little more than they would have under normal circumstances. And I mean, in a lot of ways I think that people respond to that. It shows that the brand is human, that the company is human, it's a collection of other humans and that oh yeah, they are just like me. So I do want to support them. (52:48): I'm happy to buy something from them or endorse them or support them because I believe that they're doing good. (52:58): And I think that when you feel, if you felt betrayed or you felt like, oh, this guy's schizophrenic now, you might be like, man, I don't know, they sound like weird. Or at the end of the day, they really weren't all that nice or authentic. And so I've kind of seen this darker side when things get a little tight and they acted a little different. So I just think that there's both of those give and I think, look man, we're going to look back on this year five, 10 years from now, and there's going to be all kinds of business school case studies on this stuff and we're going to learn who did it and who failed and all this and the reasons they failed. But I think from a brand perspective and those notions of transparency, staying true, being open, not acting like you have all the", "token_estimate": 1180, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Rick Wittenbraker"], "start_timestamp": "47:30", "end_timestamp": "48:04", "podcast_index": 38, "episode_name": "Ep 38: Rick Wittenbraker | Community over Self | Howler Brother‪s‬", "slug": "ep-38-rick-wittenbraker-community-over-self-howler-brothers", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-38-rick-wittenbraker-community-over-self-howler-brothers", "guest_name": "Rick Wittenbraker", "guest_title": "CMO,Howler Brothers", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Being nimble, not thinking you have the answers, being willing to adapt, these are all things that at any moment under duress, I think are opportunities for someone to not just survive, but thrive.\"", "podcast_length": "44:60"}, {"id": "10075feb-1028-410b-81e3-33e0f41d0c44", "episode_id": "8efa8786-3222-4d37-907c-69c3b145162a", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484a", "chunk_index": 15, "content": "Speaker 3 (53:59): Answers,\n\nRick Wittenbraker (54:00): Those notions are very human. And I think that humans, especially in a moment of need, respond to other humans. So I mean, shit, it's probably a life lesson, but it's a good lesson of, Hey man, here's things we can do or things that we shouldn't do once we get to the next moment of duress.\n\nCole Heilborn (54:27): Yeah, yeah, well said. At the end of the day, it's just humans connecting with humans. Yeah. Maybe it's not any more complicated than that.\n\nRick Wittenbraker (54:38): Yeah, I think honestly, I think that's a lot of it.\n\nCole Heilborn (54:42): Cool. Well, if people want to follow along with the brand, if they want to follow along with you personally and see what you guys are up to, where can people tune in?\n\nRick Wittenbraker (54:55): Well, I would say following the brand is way more interesting than following me. So the website is haller bros.com and the best thing I would tell you is to sign up for our email newsletter because they get first crack at everything. New products, new content, special promotions or things we're doing the inside scoop and that you can do on the website and then on social, on every platform is at howler bros. And please follow along. We try to have a lot of fun with it in addition to creating some great content. So I think that's a great way to follow along, especially if you've never heard of us or you don't know, it's a great way to sort, I think, get an understanding of who we are and what we act like and how we handle ourselves. And I think it's a great way to start.\n\nCole Heilborn (55:59): Yeah. Beautiful. Well, Rick, thank you so much. If you guys are interested, go ahead and follow along on the various channels that Rick mentioned. It's been fun getting to know you, Rick. Thank you for your time. It's been fun getting to know a little bit more about the brand and how you guys have weathered the last few months. So thanks for sharing and keep up the great work.\n\nRick Wittenbraker (56:20): Well, thank you so much and thanks for having me and everyone. Be sure to go on wherever you get your podcasts and leave a good review for this great work.\n\nCole Heilborn (56:29): Thank you. Alright, Rick, well have a good day.\n\nRick Wittenbraker (56:32): Alright, thank\n\nCole Heilborn (56:32): You. Bye. Thanks again for listening to this episode. If you enjoyed it, please consider sharing it with a friend and leaving us a review on Apple. Also, remember to check out Darby Communications. If you're looking for a great PR and marketing team, go ahead and find 'em at darby communications.com.", "token_estimate": 655, "speakers": ["Speaker 3", "Rick Wittenbraker", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "53:59", "end_timestamp": "56:32", "podcast_index": 38, "episode_name": "Ep 38: Rick Wittenbraker | Community over Self | Howler Brother‪s‬", "slug": "ep-38-rick-wittenbraker-community-over-self-howler-brothers", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-38-rick-wittenbraker-community-over-self-howler-brothers", "guest_name": "Rick Wittenbraker", "guest_title": "CMO,Howler Brothers", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"Being nimble, not thinking you have the answers, being willing to adapt, these are all things that at any moment under duress, I think are opportunities for someone to not just survive, but thrive.\"", "podcast_length": "44:60"}, {"id": "fc6fa193-7b74-4057-aef2-80071723fa40", "episode_id": "9022740d-9a30-45aa-9350-dfbc3d4e5aaf", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484d", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:07): Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Helebo and I'll be your host today. This podcast dives deep with leaders of the outdoor marketing industry as they discuss the gritty details of their work, personal challenges and latest adventures, whether you're standing in the lift line, shuttling to fresh loam or cracking a local beer. I hope you enjoyed today's episode. This is the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. This episode is produced by Portside Productions, an outdoor, commercial and film production company based in the Pacific Northwest. Before we jump into today's episode, I wanted to talk about Darby Communications. They're our presenting sponsor for this episode. I've gotten to know them over the last few months as they've helped me with some PR and marketing for this podcast. The Darby Comm team has designed their process around my needs, combining a people first mindset with an analytical data-driven approach. Today's business world moves fast, but the Darby Comm team has taken the time to truly get to know me and understand my goals. They've been a great partner to collaborate with, and I'm really impressed with their well-rounded approach that generate real results that actually help bring this podcast to more years. If you're in the market for a great PR team in the outdoor wellness and fitness industry, go ahead and check 'em out@darbycommunications.com. (01:24): Welcome to the Backcountry Marketing Podcast. My name is Cole Hilborn. Today I'm sitting down with Jensen and Nikolai, the two co-founders of Obras Sunglasses. Gentlemen, how are you?\n\nJensen (01:34): Quite righteous. Doing well. Thanks for having us.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:38): Yeah,\n\nNikolai (01:38): Right. You've indeed.\n\nJensen (01:43): Where are you guys\n\nCole Heilborn (01:43): Calling in from today?\n\nJensen (01:45): I am at our headquarters, global headquarters in Clyde Hill, Washington, right here in inside of a 50-year-old barn\n\nNikolai (01:55): And I in a closed beach due to COVID in Preto, Escondido, Mexico, currently trying to avoid the federal as I run to a rock to take cover to do this podcast.", "token_estimate": 538, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Jensen", "Nikolai"], "start_timestamp": "00:07", "end_timestamp": "01:55", "podcast_index": 39, "episode_name": "Ep 39: Jensen and Nikolai | Founders Dream| Ombraz Sunglasse‪s‬", "slug": "ep-39-jensen-and-nikolai-founders-dream-ombraz-sunglasses", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-39-jensen-and-nikolai-founders-dream-ombraz-sunglasses", "guest_name": "Jensen and Nikolai", "guest_title": "Ombraz Sunglasses", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"We are not trying to shove a product down your throat in our marketing. We are trying to really tell a story about who we are not only as as company, but as people in general.\"", "podcast_length": "44:61"}, {"id": "339b4e70-30fd-48ee-aded-ef1515cb9451", "episode_id": "9022740d-9a30-45aa-9350-dfbc3d4e5aaf", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484d", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Cole Heilborn (02:09): Right on. But you guys are based down in the Bellevue area, right?\n\nNikolai (02:13): That's correct, yeah, just outside Seattle.\n\nCole Heilborn (02:16): Okay, cool. So before we kind of jump into today's episode, I'd love a little background on the company and the product that you guys make. I really appreciate you guys sending me up a pair because you look at a picture online and you're like, whoa, those are not sunglasses that I'm used to seeing. So I'd love for you guys to kind of fill the audience in for folks who aren't familiar with who you are and what you make, and let's share a little bit of the backstory too. I think that's really unique as well.\n\nNikolai (02:48): Oh, great. Yeah, so basically Obras are kind of the first of their kind armless sunglasses. So we just kind of remove those problem points that everyone experiences with sunglasses, and that is they fall off your face, they slide down your nose, they pinch your head when you wear 'em for too long or have a hat on, and then they're difficult to store, so you can't really put 'em in a pocket and they're a bag without having to worry about them breaking. So by removing those sidearms and just attaching this custom, really, really high end quality cord that we attach directly to the frame, you remove those problem points and you have kind of this sunglasses that is extremely functional, incredibly practical, super comfy, and stays in place without really giving you any problems at all.", "token_estimate": 375, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Nikolai"], "start_timestamp": "02:09", "end_timestamp": "02:48", "podcast_index": 39, "episode_name": "Ep 39: Jensen and Nikolai | Founders Dream| Ombraz Sunglasse‪s‬", "slug": "ep-39-jensen-and-nikolai-founders-dream-ombraz-sunglasses", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-39-jensen-and-nikolai-founders-dream-ombraz-sunglasses", "guest_name": "Jensen and Nikolai", "guest_title": "Ombraz Sunglasses", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"We are not trying to shove a product down your throat in our marketing. We are trying to really tell a story about who we are not only as as company, but as people in general.\"", "podcast_length": "44:61"}, {"id": "6ad1b47d-6fee-4367-a161-1002af77e30d", "episode_id": "9022740d-9a30-45aa-9350-dfbc3d4e5aaf", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484d", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Jensen (03:41): Yeah, we launched with a Kickstarter campaign back in 2018, actually. It was an Indiegogo campaign, and yeah, we kind of, Jensen had come up with this idea back in college on a trip to India, and when he got back, everyone was just obsessed with them asking about them. We went to college together, and I remember stealing them every chance I could get and having Jensen chase me down and try and wrangle them away from me, and they're a total game changer. Like Jensen said, they're super comfortable, practical, super easy and light, and we've had great success out of the gate. People have really appreciated what we've been doing because like Jensen said, these are frustrations that just about everyone has with sunglasses. So after\n\nNikolai (04:35): Sunglasses been around, sunglasses have been around, sorry Nicole to interrupt you, but they've been around for 250 years and there hasn't really been any fundamental design shift. There's hundreds of sunglasses brands, but they all really, they're only unique value proposition is maybe a lens technology or just the branding itself. But as far as a fundamental design shift, there's nothing too different. There's different materials, that sort of thing, but they all fit the same. And so omers is kind of this first of its kind of new rendition on a sunglass, and we're not even trying to be an active sunglass. It's got those active points to it, the functionality and practicality, but we've kind of intertwined that into a lifestyle look. So it, they've been really well received. And Obergs, they came about, this entire idea came about just because I wore sunglasses and they were always a frustrating thing for me, and I always broke them and lost them. And so the solution was kind of just in MacGyver fix on an expedition in India, and I just ended up tying a piece of twine to these broken sunglasses that had gotten sat on, and they ended up being a better sunglass for me than my original intact pair. And so that kind of spurred this whole idea. And yeah, they've been extremely well received.", "token_estimate": 520, "speakers": ["Jensen", "Nikolai"], "start_timestamp": "03:41", "end_timestamp": "04:35", "podcast_index": 39, "episode_name": "Ep 39: Jensen and Nikolai | Founders Dream| Ombraz Sunglasse‪s‬", "slug": "ep-39-jensen-and-nikolai-founders-dream-ombraz-sunglasses", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-39-jensen-and-nikolai-founders-dream-ombraz-sunglasses", "guest_name": "Jensen and Nikolai", "guest_title": "Ombraz Sunglasses", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"We are not trying to shove a product down your throat in our marketing. We are trying to really tell a story about who we are not only as as company, but as people in general.\"", "podcast_length": "44:61"}, {"id": "0b0b3f49-3464-41c8-a633-b2b06f62fa66", "episode_id": "9022740d-9a30-45aa-9350-dfbc3d4e5aaf", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484d", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Cole Heilborn (06:07): Right on, I was out two weekends ago. I was out fly fishing over in eastern Washington with a friend, and I was wearing your sunglasses, and I took them off and my friend made a comment. He's like, oh, I see you got some croakies on your sunglasses. And I was like, Nope, look a little closer. No croakies here. And he was like, whoa, those things are cool. So he then had to of course try them on and he quickly realized the benefit of not having those annoying.\n\nJensen (06:38): And the thing is with Croakies, they solve that one issue of them kind of sliding down and not retaining on your face, but they still don't solve most of the other issues. You still have a sidearm which can break, which can squeeze your head and cause discomfort. And so we kind of take the croque to the next level.\n\nNikolai (07:04): And that's the thing too, because it's such a simple fix for sunglasses, one of our biggest hurdles has been people just being so skeptical about them actually working and actually looking good and being comfortable because it's so simple. People are like, well, if it worked, why wouldn't it have been done before? And so that's our biggest thing, is just overcoming those skeptics. And once people get a pair in their hands, like you said, and your buddy tries them on and actually confused himself, the value that a pair of owners provides, people are just automatically sold.\n\nCole Heilborn (07:40): Yeah. So how many years into the company are you?\n\nJensen (07:43): Two years. We launched on Indiegogo in April of 2018. Our campaign wrapped up by May. We delivered our first product in December of 2018.\n\nCole Heilborn (07:57): Okay, right on. So pretty fresh. As you look towards, I guess the future, where do you want the company to go? What is the brand? What is the brand you want to create?", "token_estimate": 460, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Jensen", "Nikolai"], "start_timestamp": "06:07", "end_timestamp": "07:57", "podcast_index": 39, "episode_name": "Ep 39: Jensen and Nikolai | Founders Dream| Ombraz Sunglasse‪s‬", "slug": "ep-39-jensen-and-nikolai-founders-dream-ombraz-sunglasses", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-39-jensen-and-nikolai-founders-dream-ombraz-sunglasses", "guest_name": "Jensen and Nikolai", "guest_title": "Ombraz Sunglasses", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"We are not trying to shove a product down your throat in our marketing. We are trying to really tell a story about who we are not only as as company, but as people in general.\"", "podcast_length": "44:61"}, {"id": "b2074c7c-1e86-4752-9028-bf4c6f3a6047", "episode_id": "9022740d-9a30-45aa-9350-dfbc3d4e5aaf", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484d", "chunk_index": 4, "content": "Nikolai (08:12): Oh, that's a great question. So besides having a product, we're super proud of the brand is the most important part for us. And that's kind of our job is to direct this brand, create the vision for this brand and perfect this brand. And by doing that, we focus on having a good time enjoying life and not taking anything too seriously. But at the same time, we plant 20 trees for every pair of obras that we sell, which makes them the most carbon negative product currently available on the market, meaning we sequester or remove 330 times the carbon that it takes to produce and deliver every pair through our tree planting initiatives in Madagascar, which is mangrove trees specifically. And so that's also a huge pillar of our brand. And so we take that being ecological stewards of the planet is a vital pillar of Obras, as well as this artistic expression through our content, through making people just emotionally connected to what we're doing, what we're trying to build, as well as kind of letting them know that out there, there are companies out there that take the health of our planet very seriously and can initiate measures to offset and mitigate and create a net benefit impact for the planet around us. (09:54): And we kind of want to be the leaders in that front and set a precedence for what brands, especially brands catering to millennials, 18 to 35 year olds, which is our demographic are doing and can do right now.\n\nJensen (10:10): Yeah, that net benefit, that net positive impact on the world around us is really key. We have a very purpose-driven product and we try to have a purpose-driven brand behind it. We try to make products that solve problems, serve a purpose. There's stuff that you need. It's a product that you need, not just a product that you want, not just another thing that's just a part of the consumer mindset. It's actually going to be kind of like a tool out when you're living your life. And the same thing goes with the brand. The brand reflects that, but it's about, like Jensen said, conscious fun.", "token_estimate": 517, "speakers": ["Nikolai", "Jensen"], "start_timestamp": "08:12", "end_timestamp": "10:10", "podcast_index": 39, "episode_name": "Ep 39: Jensen and Nikolai | Founders Dream| Ombraz Sunglasse‪s‬", "slug": "ep-39-jensen-and-nikolai-founders-dream-ombraz-sunglasses", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-39-jensen-and-nikolai-founders-dream-ombraz-sunglasses", "guest_name": "Jensen and Nikolai", "guest_title": "Ombraz Sunglasses", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"We are not trying to shove a product down your throat in our marketing. We are trying to really tell a story about who we are not only as as company, but as people in general.\"", "podcast_length": "44:61"}, {"id": "50bbb455-c5de-4375-aa7d-652cade78563", "episode_id": "9022740d-9a30-45aa-9350-dfbc3d4e5aaf", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484d", "chunk_index": 5, "content": "Cole Heilborn (10:53): Yeah, no, well said. I guess what I'm interested in is I love these types of conversations because you get a deeper inside look and a longer form narrative where you can understand who are these people that are behind the scenes working on these brands. And I always wonder if you could go back in time and an interview like Yvonne Sheard or Steve Jobs in year one or year two, what would you want to ask them? What would you want to learn? And so I'm really curious. You guys are fresh into this. You've got a product that is doing really well, you're solving a real need, people seem to love it. And back to Steve Jobs and Chenard, you look at the brands that they have built over the last few decades. I'm curious, you guys are in this position now where you have a lot of opportunity ahead of, but as you're thinking about the future, are you thinking about where this could go? Are you just so focused on the day-to-day managing quality control, managing shipments? How are you guys processing, I guess, where your business is going while still trying to make it run and come together?", "token_estimate": 279, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "10:53", "end_timestamp": "10:53", "podcast_index": 39, "episode_name": "Ep 39: Jensen and Nikolai | Founders Dream| Ombraz Sunglasse‪s‬", "slug": "ep-39-jensen-and-nikolai-founders-dream-ombraz-sunglasses", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-39-jensen-and-nikolai-founders-dream-ombraz-sunglasses", "guest_name": "Jensen and Nikolai", "guest_title": "Ombraz Sunglasses", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"We are not trying to shove a product down your throat in our marketing. We are trying to really tell a story about who we are not only as as company, but as people in general.\"", "podcast_length": "44:61"}, {"id": "65a69c39-fe7d-410a-9509-10f2624f1859", "episode_id": "9022740d-9a30-45aa-9350-dfbc3d4e5aaf", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484d", "chunk_index": 6, "content": "Nikolai (12:12): That's a fantastic question. Great question. And it's funny you mentioned Yvan because I don't know for your listeners that don't know, that's the founder of Patagonia, and everyone probably knows that, but let my people go surfing. His book on founding Patagonia and kind of their business unusual approach has been kind of our almost like company bible in North Star. And we have all of our employees, all of our contractors, we have read that book, and we've kind of held true to their mantra and Patagonia's way of thinking, which is kind of we're not trying to pump a bunch of cash into this business and blow it up to a bunch of VC money. We're trying to grow slow, innovate smart, grow intelligently and organically and just listen to our customers and provide incredible customer service for our customers, take care of our customers, make sure that they are satisfied through every step of the process. And if they're not, go above and beyond to make sure that they are. And that's our mantra is just what can we do to make our customers happy? And just focusing on that slow growth mentality that creates kind of that foundation for a very solid brand.", "token_estimate": 295, "speakers": ["Nikolai"], "start_timestamp": "12:12", "end_timestamp": "12:12", "podcast_index": 39, "episode_name": "Ep 39: Jensen and Nikolai | Founders Dream| Ombraz Sunglasse‪s‬", "slug": "ep-39-jensen-and-nikolai-founders-dream-ombraz-sunglasses", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-39-jensen-and-nikolai-founders-dream-ombraz-sunglasses", "guest_name": "Jensen and Nikolai", "guest_title": "Ombraz Sunglasses", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"We are not trying to shove a product down your throat in our marketing. We are trying to really tell a story about who we are not only as as company, but as people in general.\"", "podcast_length": "44:61"}, {"id": "f9c8a1a7-a1b4-40db-ad84-255041abee3e", "episode_id": "9022740d-9a30-45aa-9350-dfbc3d4e5aaf", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484d", "chunk_index": 7, "content": "Jensen (13:46): In terms of how we focus on steering the brand, we rely on a number of things. So we have a lot of great mentors that we talk to just about. Anytime we're presented with a difficult decision, we first acknowledge that this is a very difficult decision, and there's other people out there who have been confronted with these kinds of decisions. So we just reach out to the smartest people we know, pick their brains, get a collective opinion on something, and then kind of follow our instincts and are kind of own mantras on executing and making that decision. With Obras. We also just, Jensen and I both, we really value quality. There's a lot of crap out there, and we try to just put our best foot forward in everything we do, whether it's content, whether it's product, whether it's customer service, customer experience. We try to make things easy for the customer. At the end of the day, we treat ourselves like our biggest customer in a lot of different ways when we're rolling something out and we're testing it out at first, if there's any kind of discomfort, if there's any kind of anything that makes it more difficult or any frustrating in any way, we try to be as hypercritical on that as possible and try to solve that before we roll it out to the rest of our customers.\n\nCole Heilborn (15:19): So it sounds like you're not so focused on the future, but you're really just focused on making the best decisions that you can that are in front of you.\n\nNikolai (15:29): I think by making those good decisions and really being hypercritical of the entire every step of the process, you're almost paving the way for a sustainable, potentially high reward future. And there's so much crap out there nowadays. There's so many brands and there's so much junk. And that's the last thing we want to do is create a product that is mostly petrol based and just have it break and get thrown away. That's not what we're about. We're about creating a product that has longevity. It doesn't have a planned obsolescence. We want this to be the last price sunglasses you ever buy, and if something fails on it, we're going to fix it rather than throw it out.", "token_estimate": 543, "speakers": ["Jensen", "Cole Heilborn", "Nikolai"], "start_timestamp": "13:46", "end_timestamp": "15:29", "podcast_index": 39, "episode_name": "Ep 39: Jensen and Nikolai | Founders Dream| Ombraz Sunglasse‪s‬", "slug": "ep-39-jensen-and-nikolai-founders-dream-ombraz-sunglasses", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-39-jensen-and-nikolai-founders-dream-ombraz-sunglasses", "guest_name": "Jensen and Nikolai", "guest_title": "Ombraz Sunglasses", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"We are not trying to shove a product down your throat in our marketing. We are trying to really tell a story about who we are not only as as company, but as people in general.\"", "podcast_length": "44:61"}, {"id": "475db04b-50a1-4ef9-ad53-925ff53337d2", "episode_id": "9022740d-9a30-45aa-9350-dfbc3d4e5aaf", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484d", "chunk_index": 8, "content": "Cole Heilborn (16:18): So as you're, I think one of the things that's interesting too, and I'm hoping you guys can provide some context or just information around it, is how you guys talk about the company that you're wanting to build. How do you think about it? How do you put language behind it? You mentioned that you have every person that you work with reads, let my people go surfing. What are some other ways that you, either between yourselves as co-founders or with the rest of your team, help everyone get on the same page and essentially get on board with where the direction's going so that everyone understands where it's going and your guys's philosophy behind the business?\n\nJensen (17:03): Well, I think first of all, we try to be very genuine and honest with everyone we work with, including ourselves, whether that's our customers, employees, contractors, each other. That's really the first step is being critical and honest and creating a good feedback loop from everything we do. We try to be, I don't know, Jensen, what do you think?\n\nNikolai (17:35): Yeah, I think that genuine, just being genuine is so important and authentic. It goes back to just the same goes for creating a brand as it goes to the way you interact with your factories or your manufacturers or your employees or your customers. As long as you're a genuine person, you have good intentions, good things will come of it, and you're growing and building the right way.", "token_estimate": 365, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Jensen", "Nikolai"], "start_timestamp": "16:18", "end_timestamp": "17:35", "podcast_index": 39, "episode_name": "Ep 39: Jensen and Nikolai | Founders Dream| Ombraz Sunglasse‪s‬", "slug": "ep-39-jensen-and-nikolai-founders-dream-ombraz-sunglasses", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-39-jensen-and-nikolai-founders-dream-ombraz-sunglasses", "guest_name": "Jensen and Nikolai", "guest_title": "Ombraz Sunglasses", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"We are not trying to shove a product down your throat in our marketing. We are trying to really tell a story about who we are not only as as company, but as people in general.\"", "podcast_length": "44:61"}, {"id": "c91989bb-db51-4128-9243-6767468bc1ed", "episode_id": "9022740d-9a30-45aa-9350-dfbc3d4e5aaf", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484d", "chunk_index": 9, "content": "Jensen (18:06): And that's, at the end of the day, we know that we have a superior product. Ultimately, we're building something that is more comfortable, more durable, better in so many ways than the traditional sense, and it's reducing waste and it's offsetting more carbon than it emits. And we just try to do the right thing when it comes to all aspects of our business just about. And that naturally attracts the right types of people. And when we're interacting with our customers, we're trying to add value, not sell them. We'd rather have a good friendly conversation with someone than have a sales pitch, a successful sales pitch that results in them buying a few pairs of sunglasses. That's just more interesting to us. That's the way we like to live our lives is it's more of just creating those good relationships. And success comes from that very naturally and organically\n\nNikolai (19:06): That goes with the way we kind market owners as well online in our ads. We're not trying to just shove product down your throat and our videos and our imagery. It's more about harnessing the emotional tie and the feelings around what home Brew entails and who we are as a brand and as co-founders, well as just people. We are a customer. We talk to our customers and we train our customer service teams to talk to our customers the way that we would want to be talked to and the way that we kind of talk to a friend, because once someone invests in a pair of ERs, it's almost like they're part of this armless, sunglass family where they get it. You run into someone on the street wearing a pair of obras, and we've heard these stories countless times where people are like, oh, yes, you get it. Our customers will find each other wearing them. And they're like, you get it. These are freaking awesome. And so that's kind of like what we're trying to just encompass and have permeate down from top to bottom through our company, that genuine, authentic feel.", "token_estimate": 491, "speakers": ["Jensen", "Nikolai"], "start_timestamp": "18:06", "end_timestamp": "19:06", "podcast_index": 39, "episode_name": "Ep 39: Jensen and Nikolai | Founders Dream| Ombraz Sunglasse‪s‬", "slug": "ep-39-jensen-and-nikolai-founders-dream-ombraz-sunglasses", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-39-jensen-and-nikolai-founders-dream-ombraz-sunglasses", "guest_name": "Jensen and Nikolai", "guest_title": "Ombraz Sunglasses", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"We are not trying to shove a product down your throat in our marketing. We are trying to really tell a story about who we are not only as as company, but as people in general.\"", "podcast_length": "44:61"}, {"id": "aaf85bcc-83af-4952-b99a-19ae5bb8d399", "episode_id": "9022740d-9a30-45aa-9350-dfbc3d4e5aaf", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484d", "chunk_index": 10, "content": "Jensen (20:25): And it's worked well so far. It really does permeate down like that through our customers. I ran into a few people in the mountains, these backpacking this past summer, and one of them was wearing a pair of obra, and I came up to him and I was like, Hey, is that a pair of obra? And he just started raving about them and basically going through all the selling points. And I didn't tell him that I was one of the co-founders quite yet, but he just started selling me on them, just telling me about the brand, how stoked he is, the great experience he had working with our customer service and just using the product. I was like, man, it just was music to my ears when I told him I was one of the co-founders and he was extremely stoked to meet me. And it felt like a very genuine family vibe all around. And that's what we're trying to create ultimately.\n\nCole Heilborn (21:23): Yeah, that's awesome. And I think that's one of the things that I like about the outdoor industry is it seems like it's just a bunch of people trying to do their thing who are just cool humans who are having a good time. And if there's a little business that happens as well, then that's cool as well. I love what you guys said about you're not trying to pound the pavement, shove product down people's throat. I don't think that works very well, and I don't think that will work well at all in the future as consumers get smarter and as people become maybe more desensitized to traditional advertising or things like that. Yeah,\n\nJensen (22:10): It just doesn't feel good.\n\nCole Heilborn (22:11): Yeah, yeah. Elaborate on that a little bit more.", "token_estimate": 411, "speakers": ["Jensen", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "20:25", "end_timestamp": "22:11", "podcast_index": 39, "episode_name": "Ep 39: Jensen and Nikolai | Founders Dream| Ombraz Sunglasse‪s‬", "slug": "ep-39-jensen-and-nikolai-founders-dream-ombraz-sunglasses", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-39-jensen-and-nikolai-founders-dream-ombraz-sunglasses", "guest_name": "Jensen and Nikolai", "guest_title": "Ombraz Sunglasses", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"We are not trying to shove a product down your throat in our marketing. We are trying to really tell a story about who we are not only as as company, but as people in general.\"", "podcast_length": "44:61"}, {"id": "7747c669-800c-4d69-9641-24edd9dc2ada", "episode_id": "9022740d-9a30-45aa-9350-dfbc3d4e5aaf", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484d", "chunk_index": 11, "content": "Jensen (22:15): Well, yeah, it's just that feeling. It all comes back down to genuine, to being genuine, and we're trying to have our interactions with people be interactions you would have with a friend rather than interactions you'd have with a company or a brand or something inanimate. It's really all about being friendly, being genuine, building relationships. We manage our own social media accounts, our own Instagram, and we highly encourage people to reach out to us, dm us, share their stories, ask us questions, because we just genuinely love interacting with our customers and saying what's up, and hearing their stories and seeing what they're using their own for. And yeah, it fuels us, it energizes us, propels us to just want to work harder and do more and develop better stuff and gives us a chance to connect with people and get feedback as well.\n\nCole Heilborn (23:23): Yeah, no, well said. So as you look, I know you're not super focused on the future, but obviously the future well is tomorrow when you look towards the future, you think about it, does it seem clear? Does it seem like you understand where you might be pointed? Is it this kind of dark cloud that you're like, I don't really know what it looks like? What does that look like for you guys personally? And I guess how do you process through that?", "token_estimate": 334, "speakers": ["Jensen", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "22:15", "end_timestamp": "23:23", "podcast_index": 39, "episode_name": "Ep 39: Jensen and Nikolai | Founders Dream| Ombraz Sunglasse‪s‬", "slug": "ep-39-jensen-and-nikolai-founders-dream-ombraz-sunglasses", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-39-jensen-and-nikolai-founders-dream-ombraz-sunglasses", "guest_name": "Jensen and Nikolai", "guest_title": "Ombraz Sunglasses", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"We are not trying to shove a product down your throat in our marketing. We are trying to really tell a story about who we are not only as as company, but as people in general.\"", "podcast_length": "44:61"}, {"id": "8271bd50-bc8d-4512-b169-b5db74384c91", "episode_id": "9022740d-9a30-45aa-9350-dfbc3d4e5aaf", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484d", "chunk_index": 12, "content": "Jensen (23:59): There are definitely challenges with thinking about the future. Right now we're a two to four person company, so Jensen and I are pretty much the only ones full-time, still in the weeds a lot, and we are handling 35 different things every day. So it's really easy to get down in the weeds and work on that kind of stuff, that kind daily operational stuff a lot. But we've been taking more time recently to take a step back and really try to plan out our future and challenge and think about the direction we want to take as we're kind of peeling back the layers of the optical market. We're seeing so much potential here. We started out really focused on backpackers and the outdoor community, and now we're seeing a market opportunity everywhere from aviation to equestrians to construction workers and ballistic side of the fence with military and stuff like that. And it's really a lot of those things they still have, everything still has to vibe with the brand that we're building and with the base and foundation that we've already laid. So it definitely does take an intentional approach in figuring out what's next, and that's from a branding perspective and from a marketing perspective, but then also from a product perspective, we are always just in touch with our customers asking for feedback and trying to think about the next generation of Obras, how", "token_estimate": 346, "speakers": ["Jensen"], "start_timestamp": "23:59", "end_timestamp": "23:59", "podcast_index": 39, "episode_name": "Ep 39: Jensen and Nikolai | Founders Dream| Ombraz Sunglasse‪s‬", "slug": "ep-39-jensen-and-nikolai-founders-dream-ombraz-sunglasses", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-39-jensen-and-nikolai-founders-dream-ombraz-sunglasses", "guest_name": "Jensen and Nikolai", "guest_title": "Ombraz Sunglasses", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"We are not trying to shove a product down your throat in our marketing. We are trying to really tell a story about who we are not only as as company, but as people in general.\"", "podcast_length": "44:61"}, {"id": "d31bbb65-ed2d-466d-9867-659f1a0f637c", "episode_id": "9022740d-9a30-45aa-9350-dfbc3d4e5aaf", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484d", "chunk_index": 13, "content": "Nikolai (25:50): Can we create that better product? Our first version was a hand stitched pair that we made 5,000 units in our barn office that Nikolai is in, and every adjustable system was hand tied by us, and it took us almost three months to make our first 5,000 pairs working 12 to 14 hours a day, seven days a week. And that was just like, it made us so intimately familiar with the process, but it also gave us a good understanding of how we need to innovate and what we need to do to create a scalable product that can potentially someday reach the masses. And I think what Nicola touched on has been one of the most difficult parts of exploring growth and tapping into the new markets because like you said, we were focusing on that backpacking, outdoor hiking community, and we're still very focused on it. (26:54): And it's tricky because we're so small and we have really not even tapped into a fraction of that community, specifically the backpacking hiking community that where we're avid backpackers and hikers and still the most at home on the trail because they have all their aforementioned benefits. And it's been tricky because we want to, oh, what's the next market we can go after? Wow, these are so good for golfing, they're so great for pilots, yada, yada yada. But just staying focused and being like, no, this is where we need to be. Once you cast that net too wide, your message gets diluted, and I like to assimilate it to fishing. When you go fishing, you don't have a ton of lures on one line trying to catch whatever you can. You're going for one specific fish with one specific lure. And when you've been fishing with one lure for so long, you get a little bit, I don't want to say bored, but you just feel like you need to be moving on, but maybe you just need to move to a different location and fish for that fish and somewhere else. So for us, it's been tricky just being, staying focused and being like, we need to dial in and permeate this market and drive home into this market before we reach out and try and tap into it.\n\nCole Heilborn (28:23): Yeah. Well said. That analogy is really helpful. So then for you guys, when do you add more lures to the line?", "token_estimate": 549, "speakers": ["Nikolai", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "25:50", "end_timestamp": "28:23", "podcast_index": 39, "episode_name": "Ep 39: Jensen and Nikolai | Founders Dream| Ombraz Sunglasse‪s‬", "slug": "ep-39-jensen-and-nikolai-founders-dream-ombraz-sunglasses", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-39-jensen-and-nikolai-founders-dream-ombraz-sunglasses", "guest_name": "Jensen and Nikolai", "guest_title": "Ombraz Sunglasses", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"We are not trying to shove a product down your throat in our marketing. We are trying to really tell a story about who we are not only as as company, but as people in general.\"", "podcast_length": "44:61"}, {"id": "470eeed1-a9f6-4015-bb7a-2cab6593ce56", "episode_id": "9022740d-9a30-45aa-9350-dfbc3d4e5aaf", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484d", "chunk_index": 14, "content": "Jensen (28:32): When the time feels right, when it feels right?\n\nNikolai (28:35): Yeah. And we're not there yet. Once these are, once you go hiking and backpacking and you see these once every time you're on a trail, I feel like Ben, it could be time to move on. But it's tricky too because we want to change it up. We know how often these are for pilots, we know how great these are in so many different areas. So it's a difficult challenge to say the least, to just remain focused and stay just, it's like we feel like we've tapped into that market, but in reality we haven't even come close to it. So it's tricky.\n\nCole Heilborn (29:19): Yeah. I got to imagine from your perspective, you're young, you're fresh into the company, you're like, people love our product. There's so many places it could go, we have so much opportunity, let's do it all at once. But how do you stay focused? How do you stay the course and not get distracted?", "token_estimate": 235, "speakers": ["Jensen", "Nikolai", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "28:32", "end_timestamp": "29:19", "podcast_index": 39, "episode_name": "Ep 39: Jensen and Nikolai | Founders Dream| Ombraz Sunglasse‪s‬", "slug": "ep-39-jensen-and-nikolai-founders-dream-ombraz-sunglasses", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-39-jensen-and-nikolai-founders-dream-ombraz-sunglasses", "guest_name": "Jensen and Nikolai", "guest_title": "Ombraz Sunglasses", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"We are not trying to shove a product down your throat in our marketing. We are trying to really tell a story about who we are not only as as company, but as people in general.\"", "podcast_length": "44:61"}, {"id": "cd0fa723-2d24-43ed-b142-62a32ef999c7", "episode_id": "9022740d-9a30-45aa-9350-dfbc3d4e5aaf", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484d", "chunk_index": 15, "content": "Jensen (29:37): I think a lot of that, just to touch on everything else we were just talking about, a lot of it is the natural spread that our customers do on our behalf. When you think back on that, let my people go surfing model and how Patagonia started out as a climbing hardware brand, and then they started making some clothes and they noticed that people, the climbers were also skiers and snowboarders in the winter, and they were bikers and they were surfers. And that kind of naturally just progressed their brand, and naturally their brand just evolved along with their customers. And as their customers kept using their products for more and more things and letting them know how awesome their products were for all these different things that kind of propelled the brand to naturally go and explore those other avenues and niches and paths with their customers. (30:38): So I think that's kind of the approach that we're taking is just keeping our ears open and our eyes open, seeing what people are buying these for. And right now it's for the most part, it's to enjoy the outdoors. It's to in the outdoors. And we're seeing people reach out to us all the time with new stuff that they're trying these out for, and we're definitely listening to that stuff and we're kind of incorporating bits and elements of that into our marketing and into our brand and into our ethos. But it's really just like, yeah, we're letting our customers take the lead with, we have our North Star, we have our foundation. And now it's like the word is starting to spread and it's kind of starting to froth out a little bit.\n\nCole Heilborn (31:27): Yeah, no, that's so cool. So it's more about for you guys as you're trying to get a lay of the land, like letting the customer drive where you go, and then as they start to populate into these other industries, then you guys start to follow. Is that what you're saying?", "token_estimate": 478, "speakers": ["Jensen", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "29:37", "end_timestamp": "31:27", "podcast_index": 39, "episode_name": "Ep 39: Jensen and Nikolai | Founders Dream| Ombraz Sunglasse‪s‬", "slug": "ep-39-jensen-and-nikolai-founders-dream-ombraz-sunglasses", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-39-jensen-and-nikolai-founders-dream-ombraz-sunglasses", "guest_name": "Jensen and Nikolai", "guest_title": "Ombraz Sunglasses", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"We are not trying to shove a product down your throat in our marketing. We are trying to really tell a story about who we are not only as as company, but as people in general.\"", "podcast_length": "44:61"}, {"id": "a745ec8b-c026-4f3d-90b4-eb395c95b7c6", "episode_id": "9022740d-9a30-45aa-9350-dfbc3d4e5aaf", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484d", "chunk_index": 16, "content": "Jensen (31:45): Yeah, we have our own instincts and we have our own foundation and our own focus that we'd like to maintain, but as our customers are really there, they're the ones out there using these for tens of thousands of things collectively. So we're always keeping our ears open to that.\n\nCole Heilborn (32:04): Jensen, did you want to add anything?\n\nNikolai (32:05): Oh, no, I was just saying we have, listening to our customers is key, but staying true, we know the course and it's just going with our gut and steering that ship that is obras with once enough people will say something, maybe it's time to consider it, but it takes a while. We're slow moving. Like I said, we're trying to grow slow and we're trying to grow smart. And that's how you do it.\n\nCole Heilborn (32:45): Yeah, well\n\nJensen (32:46): Said. I'll give you an example. So when we first started out, we were really focusing on road tripping, backpacking, hiking, adventure, travel type of stuff. It wasn't long before a number of people reached out to us about how great these are for cycling and for how well they work with a helmet. And that kind of brought in a whole nother cycling aspect to and mountain biking and graveling and bike packing aspect to the brand. And we've integrated that a lot. If you go to our homepage right now on obergs.com, you'll see a really fantastic shot of a guy named Ultra Romance is this is Instagram handle. He is a legend in the bike packing industry, and we've definitely incorporated that into our brand. A lot of our customers also happen to be climbers, rock climbers, and so they started loved our sunglasses in the way that they worked with a helmet. They wouldn't interfere. You could take them on and off and hang them around your neck without having to worry about dropping them or having them fall off. And so that kind of bubbled out to include the climbing market as well. And as those kinds of organic progressions happen as they make sense, we're incorporating them into the brand.", "token_estimate": 504, "speakers": ["Jensen", "Cole Heilborn", "Nikolai"], "start_timestamp": "31:45", "end_timestamp": "32:46", "podcast_index": 39, "episode_name": "Ep 39: Jensen and Nikolai | Founders Dream| Ombraz Sunglasse‪s‬", "slug": "ep-39-jensen-and-nikolai-founders-dream-ombraz-sunglasses", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-39-jensen-and-nikolai-founders-dream-ombraz-sunglasses", "guest_name": "Jensen and Nikolai", "guest_title": "Ombraz Sunglasses", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"We are not trying to shove a product down your throat in our marketing. We are trying to really tell a story about who we are not only as as company, but as people in general.\"", "podcast_length": "44:61"}, {"id": "d0d67e4f-50b5-494f-871e-1c7f331b14f6", "episode_id": "9022740d-9a30-45aa-9350-dfbc3d4e5aaf", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484d", "chunk_index": 17, "content": "Cole Heilborn (34:22): Yeah, beautiful. You could ask, I dunno if you could ask any co-founder, founder of any brand or any, I don't know, marketing veteran who's been in the game for a long time, if you could learn anything or ask them anything, what would you want to ask? For example, Yvonne, what would you want to learn as two guys who are trying to figure this out for themselves?\n\nNikolai (34:50): I mean, for us, inventory management has been a really tricky kind of obstacle, if you will, to nail down and because been out of stock both summers now during peaks and last season. And so I guess just anything regarding how do you forecast with no data as a growing brand with high demand and little cache to, I think that goes with that slow growth mentality that Yvonne was true to in the beginning of just self-funding and being demand, but also we don't want to limit our potential by our inventory constraints. So I guess just refining that and dialing it in, getting their perspective on how they did that initially would help us. I think as well as just growing your company and making the first hires, we've struggled with bringing someone else on full time, what would that role be? What would it look like? How do you set that up? I think that's all just really, really important information. That would be great to hear from someone like Yvonne. Michael, do you have anything to add?", "token_estimate": 351, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Nikolai"], "start_timestamp": "34:22", "end_timestamp": "34:50", "podcast_index": 39, "episode_name": "Ep 39: Jensen and Nikolai | Founders Dream| Ombraz Sunglasse‪s‬", "slug": "ep-39-jensen-and-nikolai-founders-dream-ombraz-sunglasses", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-39-jensen-and-nikolai-founders-dream-ombraz-sunglasses", "guest_name": "Jensen and Nikolai", "guest_title": "Ombraz Sunglasses", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"We are not trying to shove a product down your throat in our marketing. We are trying to really tell a story about who we are not only as as company, but as people in general.\"", "podcast_length": "44:61"}, {"id": "2551083a-bc5c-41df-a9da-299e687ea620", "episode_id": "9022740d-9a30-45aa-9350-dfbc3d4e5aaf", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484d", "chunk_index": 18, "content": "Jensen (36:14): Yeah, I would add to that I would add finding the right factory partners and manufacturing partners. That's something that we got lucky at first. We found a pretty good partner and a number of pretty good partners. But as we've grown, we've kind of started to outgrow some of our factories and just managing that relationship because ultimately when you talk about supply chain and inventory constraints, it all really comes back down to the factory and the relationship you build with them and how much the trust that you build with them, because at some point you're going to need them to kind of bend over backwards for you. And unless you have a great rapport and a solid relationship built, you have that trust that you're on the same team. They're not going to be as inclined to bend over backwards for you. So a lot of early stage growth as a company has a lot to do with who your manufacturing partners are.\n\nCole Heilborn (37:25): Yeah. Wow. No, those are good. Those are great answers. So what of my last questions for you, picture 10, 15 years down the road when the brand that you guys are building, let's just say for lack of a better way to say it, has been built, what is your biggest fear now about the brand that you might build in the future?", "token_estimate": 319, "speakers": ["Jensen", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "36:14", "end_timestamp": "37:25", "podcast_index": 39, "episode_name": "Ep 39: Jensen and Nikolai | Founders Dream| Ombraz Sunglasse‪s‬", "slug": "ep-39-jensen-and-nikolai-founders-dream-ombraz-sunglasses", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-39-jensen-and-nikolai-founders-dream-ombraz-sunglasses", "guest_name": "Jensen and Nikolai", "guest_title": "Ombraz Sunglasses", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"We are not trying to shove a product down your throat in our marketing. We are trying to really tell a story about who we are not only as as company, but as people in general.\"", "podcast_length": "44:61"}, {"id": "f26342e2-c77b-4d68-823e-765a70273d39", "episode_id": "9022740d-9a30-45aa-9350-dfbc3d4e5aaf", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484d", "chunk_index": 19, "content": "Nikolai (37:50): I mean, I wouldn't say fear, but I would say something we want to be aware of is not losing track and not losing that artistic expression and that creative wherewithal that we put into the brand. Not only is this a really great product, do we have a lot of fun talking to our customers and building, but it's also just that creativity we get to put into it. As far as product design goes, as far as imagery goes, as far as videography goes, as far as our copy goes, we take that creative expression and that artistic, it's just a great, what's the word I for? I want to say outlook, but I think that's very important for any human is to kind of exercise the creative part of their brain. For us, this allows us to do so in a way that lucrative and creates a job for us that we love doing. We love working, and I don't think anyone I know, anyone we know at this point has, we feel like we have the best job in the world right now. And I think as long as we can feel like that throughout this entire process of building the brand, we're going to be doing the right thing.\n\nJensen (39:23): Yeah, I'll just elaborate on that as well. So as you scale anything, it becomes more difficult to maintain those genuine artisan feels about it. When you're worried about a couple hundred units, it's much easier to have to be hands-on with every single one of those units and spend all the time and give them all the love that they need to be special. When you start worrying about thousands of units and global distribution and things start kind of getting more out of your hands because it's just not feasible anymore. And so that's one of the things that we just want to make sure we keep solidly ingrained in our business and our mantra is just making sure we can keep that deity and keep that special touch that makes numbers what it is right now.", "token_estimate": 465, "speakers": ["Nikolai", "Jensen"], "start_timestamp": "37:50", "end_timestamp": "39:23", "podcast_index": 39, "episode_name": "Ep 39: Jensen and Nikolai | Founders Dream| Ombraz Sunglasse‪s‬", "slug": "ep-39-jensen-and-nikolai-founders-dream-ombraz-sunglasses", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-39-jensen-and-nikolai-founders-dream-ombraz-sunglasses", "guest_name": "Jensen and Nikolai", "guest_title": "Ombraz Sunglasses", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"We are not trying to shove a product down your throat in our marketing. We are trying to really tell a story about who we are not only as as company, but as people in general.\"", "podcast_length": "44:61"}, {"id": "a40a6152-41c1-4f75-abe3-735bbb1886c6", "episode_id": "9022740d-9a30-45aa-9350-dfbc3d4e5aaf", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484d", "chunk_index": 20, "content": "Nikolai (40:30): We've so far as a company, as a brand, we've sold X amount of units and Nikolai and I have hand inspected every single pair that's gone out the door. Every pair that you see on the street, every pair that's in REI, every pair that's on our website, every pair that we've sold, we have had in our hands looked over and given it the seal of approval to go out the door. And so that's special. And while not feasible, like Nikolai said, to continue doing, we want to remain true to kind of that ethos and that mentality as well. In addition to biggest fear right now, OBRA is the only real sunglass, armless, sunglass on the market. And I think we need to just innovate smart and build a really solid brand to make sure that we stay dominating that sector and that field and that little niche market of sunglasses because we kind of want to be like that Kleenex of tissue, the armless, the obras of armless, if you will.\n\nCole Heilborn (41:48): Yeah. I mean it's just a matter of time before copycat shows up and copies what you're doing if it\n\nNikolai (41:54): We market. Yeah. And we're patented and we have been knocked off and we're currently suing them. And so yeah, it happens, but we have good intellectual property protecting us and we want to make sure that we kind of own this market right now.\n\nCole Heilborn (42:15): Yeah. Cool guys. Well, we're about out of time for today's episode, but I want to thank you for sitting down and chatting. It's been really cool to just understand a little bit more about how you guys are thinking, how you're processing, what are the challenges you're facing, and who knows, maybe when you look back in five years on this episode, you'd be like, Hey, remember when we said that? That was funny. We solved that four years ago. That's\n\nNikolai (42:41): Really funny. I was just sitting here thinking, this will be fun to have in five to 10 years. We can look back and kind of see where our head was at. So yeah, thank you for having us and thank you for the great questions and steering the course of this conversation. That was really, really fun for us.\n\nJensen (43:01): Yeah, cool. We really appreciate it. Thank you.", "token_estimate": 545, "speakers": ["Nikolai", "Cole Heilborn", "Jensen"], "start_timestamp": "40:30", "end_timestamp": "43:01", "podcast_index": 39, "episode_name": "Ep 39: Jensen and Nikolai | Founders Dream| Ombraz Sunglasse‪s‬", "slug": "ep-39-jensen-and-nikolai-founders-dream-ombraz-sunglasses", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-39-jensen-and-nikolai-founders-dream-ombraz-sunglasses", "guest_name": "Jensen and Nikolai", "guest_title": "Ombraz Sunglasses", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"We are not trying to shove a product down your throat in our marketing. We are trying to really tell a story about who we are not only as as company, but as people in general.\"", "podcast_length": "44:61"}, {"id": "b547eb60-f170-43c2-aa05-c12468a265e6", "episode_id": "9022740d-9a30-45aa-9350-dfbc3d4e5aaf", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c484d", "chunk_index": 21, "content": "Cole Heilborn (43:04): Absolutely. If people want to find Obras, where can they check you guys out?\n\nNikolai (43:12): Obras.com. That's O-M-B-R-A z.com. Obra means shade and Italian, and so it's kind of like shade for your eyes, shade for degraded landscapes. I'm Italian. Sunglasses come from Highend, sunglasses come from Italy, and you kind of have that kind of stigma. So that's where that kind of background comes from. And we may or may not be transitioning to Italy in the near future. So yeah, that's where they can find us. Obra at obra is on Instagram as well. If you guys want to shoot at the DM or just reach out there, we are happy to connect with anyone that has any questions for us or anything like that. Yeah, we look forward to talking to anyone that feels so inclined.\n\nCole Heilborn (44:00): Cool. Alright guys. Well thanks so much. Have a good rest of your day. And yeah, keep up the great work. Thanks again for listening to this episode. If you enjoyed it, please consider sharing it with a friend and leaving us a review on Apple. Also, remember to check out Darby Communications. If you're looking for a great PR and marketing team, go ahead and find 'em at darby communications.com.", "token_estimate": 302, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Nikolai"], "start_timestamp": "43:04", "end_timestamp": "44:00", "podcast_index": 39, "episode_name": "Ep 39: Jensen and Nikolai | Founders Dream| Ombraz Sunglasse‪s‬", "slug": "ep-39-jensen-and-nikolai-founders-dream-ombraz-sunglasses", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/ep-39-jensen-and-nikolai-founders-dream-ombraz-sunglasses", "guest_name": "Jensen and Nikolai", "guest_title": "Ombraz Sunglasses", "categories": [], "short_description": "\"We are not trying to shove a product down your throat in our marketing. We are trying to really tell a story about who we are not only as as company, but as people in general.\"", "podcast_length": "44:61"}, {"id": "d7e76c7f-7a1a-49ea-ace8-ca23e24c999a", "episode_id": "87fc0045-2196-4ae9-9966-4c6c1084c94e", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4827", "chunk_index": 0, "content": "Cole Heilborn (00:03): Welcome to Brand Story, the podcast where we interview individuals who are creating and designing the brands we interact with every day. My name is Cole Hoborne. Today I'm here with Michael Goldstein, the director at Goldstein Productions. How are you doing?\n\nMike Goldstein (00:14): Doing excellent.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:15): Thank you for having me today. We're here in beautiful Vancouver, bc.\n\nMike Goldstein (00:20): Yes. Yes. It's still beautiful for another day before it starts pouring rain.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:24): Right. So tell me a little about you guys. What do you guys do for those of people in the world who don't know?\n\nMike Goldstein (00:30): So we do commercial film production. A lot of our stuff is digital content, but we also do some broadcast work, and we work probably 50% with agencies, 50% directly with brands, and we're pretty heavily based in automotive lifestyle, active living, and the weld industry.\n\nCole Heilborn (00:50): Okay. Okay. And how long have you been doing it?\n\nMike Goldstein (00:54): So I've been doing it as a freelance gig starting in 2010, and then incorporated in 2013. Hired the first person in 2015, and now we're a team of four on the core team on the west coast. And then we have a Montreal partner as well.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:13): Awesome. And so your role as a director, I mean, I know you guys probably all wear a lot of hats, but if you're looking at the director role, what does that mean to you and what do you do?\n\nMike Goldstein (01:26): That's a good question. Yeah, so we definitely all do wear many hats, but if we're talking director specifically, I think it's the ability to have a vision in your head before you get into production and be able to communicate that before anything's shot to your client and then fulfill that throughout production and post.\n\nCole Heilborn (01:49): So you got started how doing mountain bike videos, tell me a little bit about that you get started.", "token_estimate": 493, "speakers": ["Cole Heilborn", "Mike Goldstein"], "start_timestamp": "00:03", "end_timestamp": "01:49", "podcast_index": 3, "episode_name": "Ep 3: Mike Goldstein | Running a Successful Production Company | The Heist", "slug": "mike-goldstein-running-a-successful-production-company-the-heist", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/mike-goldstein-running-a-successful-production-company-the-heist", "guest_name": "Michael Goldstein", "guest_title": "The Heist", "categories": [], "short_description": "In today's episode, I sit down and chat with Michael Goldstein from The Heist, about his origins shooting mountain bike videos at Whistler to now, shooting commercials for GMC and Lulu Lemon and Jaybird.", "podcast_length": "20:44"}, {"id": "fdb47ab5-0d78-46a5-af80-7d9068a53f1e", "episode_id": "87fc0045-2196-4ae9-9966-4c6c1084c94e", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4827", "chunk_index": 1, "content": "Mike Goldstein (01:56): Yeah, so I was working up in Whistler at a bike shop, loved riding, raced mountain bikes and also just loved making images, whether it be photo or video. So had started just filming all my buddies up there and made some mountain bike movies. We did a full length mountain bike movie at one point that we premiered at the local bar up there on opening weekend in Whistler. And really just through that whole community of mountain biking, got to know people at Whistler, and it was in 2010 that I was chatting to them. And this was back when online content was still pretty up and coming, I guess you could say. So they were like, yeah, we want to make some video stuff. We don't know what, we don't know what it's going to be. And we ended up doing the first ever online video campaign back in 2011, and that was kind of the start to it all.\n\nCole Heilborn (02:54): Okay. Okay. So you were describing to me earlier, taking a DV camera, wrapping it in bubble wrap to protect it.\n\nMike Goldstein (03:02): Yes. My cocaine brick, my camera.\n\nCole Heilborn (03:05): Is this before GoPro?\n\nMike Goldstein (03:07): That's a good question. Yeah, I think I remember when the GoPro three came out, there was like 2.7 K in 2009 I think it was. So this was, yeah, definitely before GoPro. And I think I remember too one, I think it was one time back ski racing where a buddy of mine got a GoPro. I remember he was like, yeah, you can stick it on your ski pole. And I was like, that's just a piece of shit. But they're really good now.\n\nCole Heilborn (03:33): They've changed a little bit since then.\n\nMike Goldstein (03:35): Totally.\n\nCole Heilborn (03:35): So one thing that I find really interesting, and I'm hoping we can kind of dissect in a brief manner, but you graduated from school and then you just kind of jumped into the commercial stuff or a progression down that path for people that are trying to figure out how to get into the commercial world, what would you say to them? What would be some of the things you've learned along the way?", "token_estimate": 516, "speakers": ["Mike Goldstein", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "01:56", "end_timestamp": "03:35", "podcast_index": 3, "episode_name": "Ep 3: Mike Goldstein | Running a Successful Production Company | The Heist", "slug": "mike-goldstein-running-a-successful-production-company-the-heist", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/mike-goldstein-running-a-successful-production-company-the-heist", "guest_name": "Michael Goldstein", "guest_title": "The Heist", "categories": [], "short_description": "In today's episode, I sit down and chat with Michael Goldstein from The Heist, about his origins shooting mountain bike videos at Whistler to now, shooting commercials for GMC and Lulu Lemon and Jaybird.", "podcast_length": "20:44"}, {"id": "17e1fd43-1471-4e09-98d5-b92f63796866", "episode_id": "87fc0045-2196-4ae9-9966-4c6c1084c94e", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4827", "chunk_index": 2, "content": "Mike Goldstein (04:01): The biggest thing I think is networking. And I think it's one of the things in school that I laughed at a lot in our first producing class that we took. They were like, yeah, networking is super important. There's this thing called LinkedIn, you got to go and meet people. And I was like, fuck that. I don't like talking to people and whatever. Many years down the road now it's like it's the most important thing. It's what we spend probably most of our time doing is meeting people, building relationships. You got to build those relationships and people need to trust you and you need to be able to communicate the work that you can do. And it takes that, I think, to get in the door with anyone.\n\nCole Heilborn (04:42): Do you feel like there's the filmmaker, photographer world, people are hesitant to go try to build those relationships and network? Is that a personality trait of a lot of creative people that they want to be focusing on their work and not out in the spotlight, so to speak?\n\nMike Goldstein (05:03): Yes and no. I don't know, maybe it's just the people that have been around. I feel like there's a fair few people that I know who are not opposed to that at all. They're pretty out there building those relationships. But I definitely do think that, I kind of see we're getting at with that. There's definitely a bit of a divide, I think, between your super creative people and people with the business minds mines. And I do see it being very tough if you can't acknowledge and see the difference and try and bring them together. It can be really tough, I guess, as a freelancer, but that's also not necessarily the only way to get into the industry either. There's a million different ways that you can enter in. But yeah,\n\nCole Heilborn (05:47): So maybe elaborate. What's number two and three on that list?", "token_estimate": 464, "speakers": ["Mike Goldstein", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "04:01", "end_timestamp": "05:47", "podcast_index": 3, "episode_name": "Ep 3: Mike Goldstein | Running a Successful Production Company | The Heist", "slug": "mike-goldstein-running-a-successful-production-company-the-heist", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/mike-goldstein-running-a-successful-production-company-the-heist", "guest_name": "Michael Goldstein", "guest_title": "The Heist", "categories": [], "short_description": "In today's episode, I sit down and chat with Michael Goldstein from The Heist, about his origins shooting mountain bike videos at Whistler to now, shooting commercials for GMC and Lulu Lemon and Jaybird.", "podcast_length": "20:44"}, {"id": "162f0d5b-5f18-4c45-95e5-5da9b20506d2", "episode_id": "87fc0045-2196-4ae9-9966-4c6c1084c94e", "cms_item_id": "68225dfc7bb476527f8c4827", "chunk_index": 3, "content": "Mike Goldstein (05:51): Well, I've got some good buddies of mine who are, they're like, I'm just directors. They have no interest in running business or any of the business stuff that you need to do to build up clients and whatnot. But if they team up with a great production company, they can get right in there and do the work that they want to be doing too. Because face it, if you don't want to do any of that producing work and you just want to direct and you just want to create and you want to write, there's no point in doing all that shit that you don't want to necessarily do.\n\nCole Heilborn (06:23): Sure, sure. Okay. Okay. So the media film commercial world is pretty populated with a lot of production companies and individuals doing stuff. What sets you guys apart and how have you guys Fuck that question, man,\n\nMike Goldstein (06:44): It's funny. That's, sorry, finish.\n\nCole Heilborn (06:47): No,\n\nMike Goldstein (06:47): Finish the\n\nCole Heilborn (06:48): Question. That's basically the gist of it.", "token_estimate": 254, "speakers": ["Mike Goldstein", "Cole Heilborn"], "start_timestamp": "05:51", "end_timestamp": "06:48", "podcast_index": 3, "episode_name": "Ep 3: Mike Goldstein | Running a Successful Production Company | The Heist", "slug": "mike-goldstein-running-a-successful-production-company-the-heist", "web_url": "https://www.portsidepro.com/podcast-feed/mike-goldstein-running-a-successful-production-company-the-heist", "guest_name": "Michael Goldstein", "guest_title": "The Heist", "categories": [], "short_description": "In today's episode, I sit down and chat with Michael Goldstein from The Heist, about his origins shooting mountain bike videos at Whistler to now, shooting commercials for GMC and Lulu Lemon and Jaybird.", "podcast_length": "20:44"}]
